	// already inside `SemanticCheck.calleeNeedsWin32Substrate`, so a wasm/linux/arm64 program that reaches
	// `Directory.list` is refused (E3104) at ITS OWN call site, while a program that never calls one is
	// unaffected on every target. Listing the module is what makes those calls exist; it is not what gates
	// them — which is why `specs-shv2/directory.md` carries the same `<!-- targets: x64-windows -->` marker
	// `file-io.md` documents, and points at that file for the reason.
	try listWhitelistedModule(stdlibDir, relative: "Directory.maxon", into: listed)
	// ⭐⭐ **BATCH2 slice 6 — THE THREE BYTE-WALKING `helpers/string/` MODULES, ONE ENTRY IN THREE LINES.**
	// `utf8.maxon` gives the corpus's own UTF-8 primitives (`utf8ByteLengthAt`, `utf8DecodeAt`, the four byte
	// classifiers, `utf8EncodeLength`, `utf8IsValidCodepoint`); `hash.maxon` gives `hashString`, the djb2 walk
	// `Map with (String, V)` will call on every insert; `grapheme.maxon` gives the UAX #29 segmenter WRITTEN IN
	// MAXON (`countGraphemes`, `findGraphemeEnd`/`findGraphemeStart`, the two classifiers, the index
	// conversions). `grapheme.maxon` calls the other two, which is why the three are one entry.
	//
	// ⚠ **ALL THREE STOPPED AT ONE MISSING METHOD, AND IT WAS THE SAME ONE.** `String.byteAt` — the THROWING
	// twin of `byteAtOrPanic`, which BATCH2 slice 1 already served — is how every byte walker outside
	// `stdlib/String.maxon` reaches a String's bytes, and its own doc says why it takes the String rather than
	// the `__ManagedMemory`: a `__ManagedMemory` parameter would MATERIALIZE a record per call once a small
	// String becomes a 16-byte value, i.e. one allocation per codepoint decoded. It was served by
	// `__str_byte_at`, stdlib-only at the parser exactly as the other two byte doors were.
	// ⚠ **W49 wave 7 RETIRED IT AND DELETED THAT ENTRY POINT**: `String.byteAt` is
	// `stdlib/String.maxon:266` now, forwarding to `__ManagedMemory.byteAt`, which carries the identical
	// bound and the identical `managedMemoryThrows()` clause. The reason recorded above — that the method
	// takes the String rather than the `__ManagedMemory` — is the corpus's own and is untouched by that.
	//
