	// ⭐⭐ **`stdlib/Log.maxon` — AND IT IS THE ENTRY THAT FOUND THE INVARIANT'S HOLE, `S2g`+`S2h`.**
	// `Log.trace(key)` plus the capture trio: a trace-key recorder for stdlib instrumentation, not a
	// logger. It is the callee the six `helpers/sort/*` files name (`Log.trace`, e.g.
	// `driftsort.maxon:154`), so listing it is the first half of making those bodies reachable at all.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **IT IS THE FIRST LISTED MODULE WITH MUTABLE TOP-LEVEL STATE, AND ON ITS FIRST ATTEMPT IT BROKE
	// THIS FILE'S BYTE-NEUTRALITY INVARIANT OUTRIGHT.** `var capturing = false` and
	// `var captured = TraceKeyArray.create()` (`:26-27`). MEASURED by a same-worktree A/B, one trivial
	// program, two binaries: `function main() returns ExitCode / return 7` emitted **1,592 code bytes
	// without this entry and 2,978 with it — +1,386, +87%** — in a program that never spells `Log`.
	// The entry was WITHDRAWN rather than landed, and the blocker filed as `S2h`.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE CAUSE WAS NOT THIS MODULE — IT WAS THAT THE TWO MECHANISMS KEEPING AN UNUSED MODULE FREE
	// PRUNE THE WRONG THINGS.** DFE prunes FUNCTIONS; `StdlibFacts.unreachable` prunes LOWERING; a
	// top-level `var` with an initializer runs in `__module_init`, which is NEITHER.
	// `DeadGlobalElimination` had no reachability term at all, and its own header had predicted this exact
	// case by name while asserting it was unreachable *"in any case"* — both halves of that reason had
	// expired. `S2h` gave the pass the term. **Re-measured after it: 1,592 with this entry — Δ0.**
	// ⇒ **the entry is listed here only because that A/B now reads zero**, and any future entry for a
	// module with top-level state owes the same measurement rather than the same argument.
	//
	// It clears every entry in this file's collision rule, each CHECKED rather than assumed:
	//   • FREE FUNCTIONS (E3006): it declares NONE — all four are `static`s of `Log` — so the clause, and
	//     with it the SILENT bare-builtin shadow that cost `Sleep.maxon` a builtin retirement, is vacuous.
	//     Checked against `Parser.parseCallNamed`'s bare-name `if` chain AT THE CHAIN, in BOTH directions:
	//     it declares nothing the chain can see, and it CONSUMES nothing the chain claims — every call in
	//     its body is receiver-dot on an `Array` (`push`/`clear`/`clone`).
	//   • TYPE NAMES: `Log` is nominal, so a user program spelling it wins and this one moves into the
	//     reserved space (A1s wave 2, directory-scoped by N1). No other listed module declares it, checked
	//     AT THE SOURCE rather than against a roster transcribed here.
	//   • TYPE-NAME-vs-BUILTIN, the one shape still SILENT: `Log` is on neither
	//     `TypeResolution.isCompilerOwnedTypeName` nor `builtinTypeNameTag`, and the spelling appears
	//     NOWHERE in shv2's own compiler sources as a type, so nothing synthesizes a declaration of it.
	//   • `TraceKeyArray = Array with String` (`:24`) is a GENERIC-INSTANCE alias, file-local per alias
	//     FORM exactly as `CommandLine`'s `StringArray` is — NOT ranged. This module declares no ranged
	//     alias at all, so the clause that moved `File.maxon`'s golden through a declared `Byte` has
	//     nothing to weigh.
	//   • The "type-parameterized by a user declaration" clause: its body passes no `__ManagedMemory`.
	//   • E3112 fires only when a user program actually contests a spelling; the surface is four method
	//     names (`trace`, `startCapture`, `stopCapture`, `fired`) and two labels (`key`, `capturedKeys`).
	//   • It has NO imports and calls into no other stdlib module.
	//   • ⭐⭐ **TOP-LEVEL BINDING NAMES — THE ONE CLAUSE NO ENTRY ABOVE HAS EVER EXERCISED, because this is
	//     the first listed module that HAS one, and so the one clause that could not be inherited.** A
	//     global's name is FLAT and PROJECT-WIDE (`ProgramSignatures.isRedeclaredBinding`), which is a
	//     RULING and not an implementation detail — so an EXPORTED `capturing` here would be the same name
	//     as a user's, declared twice. Neither is exported: both are file-private, so each is filed under a
	//     key carrying its declaring path (`topLevelStorageKey`) and a user program may still declare its
	//     own. **MEASURED both ways rather than read off the rule** — `var captured = "abcd"` /
	//     `var capturing = true` in a program that never mentions `Log` compiles and answers 4; a program
	//     declaring its own `captured` array AND driving `Log.startCapture`/`trace`/`stopCapture` keeps the
	//     two apart and answers 3. ⇒ **an entry for a module with an EXPORTED top-level binding owes an
	//     E3006 argument this one does not.**
	//   • Its two `.data` labels are minted from the bare NAMES (`uniqueDataLabel`), and the arena's order
	//     decides which claimant of a name gets the unsuffixed label — so listing a module with top-level
	//     state is the one kind of entry that could rename a USER global's emitted symbol. MEASURED: it does
	//     not. A user `var captured` still emits `__data_captured`, from a source path sorting both before
	//     and after the checkout's `stdlib/`, because the root's own declarations are swept first.
	//   • ⚠ It is what makes `storedGlobalCount` non-zero on EVERY compile, which permanently retires
	//     `DeadGlobalElimination.liveGlobalLabels`' `storedGlobals == 0` early-return. That is a per-compile
	//     CPU cost with no allocation behind it, so it is invisible to the memory columns — see that
	//     function's header, where the consequence is written down beside the guard it disarms.
	try listWhitelistedModule(stdlibDir, relative: "Log.maxon", into: listed)
	// ⭐ `stdlib/Ascii.maxon` — `Ascii.isDigit`/`isAlpha`/`isAlphanumeric`/`isWhitespace`/`isUpper`/
	// `isLower`, six ASCII classifiers written as range `match`es over a `Character`. Pure Maxon, no
	// intrinsic and no backend work: the module is loadable exactly as written.
	//
	// ⚠ Its blocker was `E2028 :10:4` — `match c '0' to '9'`, a pattern typed `int` against a `Character`
	// scrutinee — and BATCH23's Character rung deleted it by making every character literal a `Character`
	// and giving `Character` ordering and range patterns. No compiler change is owed here; this is the
	// whitelist line alone.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **IT IS THE FIRST ENTRY WHOSE READINESS CHECK WAS PROVED ABLE TO FAIL — AND THAT IS A RUN, NOT
	// AN ARGUMENT.** `STDLIB-BRINGUP.md`'s headline measured that the check this file prescribes
	// (`build stdlib/<M>.maxon` ⇒ `E3001` and nothing else) is VACUOUS for a module whose whole content is
	// a conditional `extension`: the compiler declines to specialize those bodies, so an undefined call
	// injected into one is never reported and the criterion passes on code nobody analyzed — true of all
	// six `helpers/sort/*` files. MEASURED here on copies outside `stdlib/`, BOTH directions: verbatim ⇒
	// `E3001` only; with `let bogus = NoSuchType.definitelyUndefined(1)` in `isDigit`'s body ⇒ `E3001`
	// **plus `E3004 call to undefined function 'NoSuchType.definitelyUndefined'`**. ⇒ the green below is
	// evidence about these six bodies, because this module is six ordinary `static function`s and not an
	// `extension` at all.
	//
	// It clears every entry in this file's collision rule, each CHECKED rather than assumed:
	//   • FREE FUNCTIONS (E3006): it declares NONE — all six are `static`s of `Ascii` — so the clause, and
	//     with it the SILENT bare-builtin shadow that cost `Sleep.maxon` a builtin retirement, is vacuous.
	//     Checked against `Parser.parseCallNamed`'s bare-name `if` chain AT THE CHAIN, as that rule's last
	//     bullet insists, and in BOTH directions: it declares nothing the chain can see, and it CONSUMES
	//     nothing the chain claims either — every call in its body is receiver-dot (`c.byteLength()`) or
	//     `Type.`-qualified (`Ascii.isAlpha`, `Ascii.isDigit`), never a bare name the way `Math.pow`
	//     consumes `floor`.
	//   • TYPE NAMES: `Ascii` is nominal, so a user program spelling it wins and this one moves into the
	//     reserved space (A1s wave 2, directory-scoped by N1). No other stdlib file declares it — checked
	//     AT THE SOURCE across all 50, not against a roster transcribed here.
	//   • ⚠⚠ **TYPE-NAME-vs-BUILTIN, the one shape still SILENT — AND THIS IS THE FIRST ENTRY WHOSE TYPE
	//     NAME shv2's OWN COMPILER SOURCE ALSO DECLARES.** `Ascii` is on neither
	//     `TypeResolution.isCompilerOwnedTypeName` nor `builtinTypeNameTag`, and nothing in the compiler
	//     synthesizes a declaration of it, so the silent shape is clear. What reading does NOT settle is
	//     `Lexer.maxon:1517`'s file-private `enum Ascii` (byte constants `underscore`, `dot`, `hash`, `at`,
	//     `quote`, `backslash`): that is an ORDINARY USER DECLARATION as far as any compiler is concerned,
	//     and it is exactly what the shadow mechanism is for — so it was MEASURED, not argued.
	//     ⚠ **And measured on declarations that DIFFER, which is the shape this file's own ⛔⛔ paragraph
	//     demands** — a control whose two declarations agree cannot tell "the user's won" from "whichever
	//     folded last won", and that is the bug it agreed with for `sleep`. A user `type Ascii` whose
	//     `isDigit` returns an **`int`** where this module's returns a **`bool`** compiles and answers 42;
	//     had the stdlib fold overwritten the user's signature it would have been a TYPE ERROR, not a
	//     wrong number. The `enum` shape shv2's own Lexer writes was run too and answers 46.
	//   • It declares NO typealias at all, ranged or otherwise, so the clause that moved `File.maxon`'s
	//     golden through a declared `Byte` has nothing to weigh. That is a declaration COUNT, not a hope.
	//   • It declares NO top-level binding, so it does not inherit the class `S2h` had to build a
	//     reachability term for: `DeadGlobalElimination` has no global of this module's to keep or drop,
	//     and `__module_init` gains no store.
	//   • The "type-parameterized by a user declaration" clause: its body passes no `__ManagedMemory` and
	//     names no type but `Character` and `bool`. ⚠ Checked as a BUILD, which is what that clause demands
	//     rather than a reading — a program declaring `typealias Byte = int(0 to 1000)` and calling nothing
	//     here compiles and runs clean.
	//   • E3112 fires only when a user program actually contests a spelling, so there is nothing to clear
	//     ahead of time. The surface is six method names and **NO argument label at all** — the file
	//     contains no `:` — and the only member spellings its own tokens carry are `.byteLength` (already
	//     `FilePath.maxon`'s), `.isAlpha` and `.isDigit`.
	//   • It has NO imports and calls into no other stdlib module.
	//
	// MEASURED before listing, which is the check this file's rule prescribes:
	// `maxon-shv2 build stdlib/Ascii.maxon` exits 1 with `E3001: No 'main' function found` AND NOTHING
	// ELSE. And the capability is real rather than merely loadable: the six classifiers driven from a user
	// program answer **127** (every bit set) under shv2 and **127** under the bootstrap oracle.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE THIRD QUESTION `S2g` ESTABLISHED — what does the entry cost a program that does NOT use it?
	// — IS ANSWERED BY AN A/B, NOT BY THE ABSENCE OF TOP-LEVEL STATE.** Zero top-level state PREDICTS a
	// zero, and `Log.maxon` is why this file measures instead of predicting. MEASURED, same worktree, two
	// binaries, `function main() returns ExitCode / return 7`: **1,592 code bytes both ways — Δ0**, and
	// stronger than the codeBytes match `S2g` recorded, the two emitted executables are **byte-for-byte
	// identical** (5,567 bytes, `cmp` clean).
	try listWhitelistedModule(stdlibDir, relative: "Ascii.maxon", into: listed)

	// ⭐⭐ **W43 — `helpers/itertools/withIterator.maxon`, AND IT IS LISTED TO CLOSE A HOLE RATHER THAN TO ADD
	// A CAPABILITY.** `Interfaces.maxon`'s `extension Iterable` declares
	// `typealias WithIterSelf = WithIterIterator with Iter, Element`, and an extension body is specialized
	// once per CONFORMER — so the first compile in which any PARSED type implements `Iterable` reports
	// **`E2055 stdlib/Interfaces.maxon:226:27  Type 'WithIterIterator' has no associated types`**, naming a
	// module nothing listed. The corpus's own `Iterable` conformers (`Array`, `String`, `List`, `Set`,
	// `Range`, `Vector`, the three String views) are all SYNTHESIZED here and so are not parsed conformers,
	// which is the only reason the hole stayed latent: **a user program writing
	// `implements Iterable with (E, I)` trips it today**, and no spec in the suite writes one.
	//
	// It clears every entry in this file's collision rule, each checked rather than assumed:
	//   • FREE FUNCTIONS (E3006): **NONE.** The file is one `type` and three of its members, so the clause —
	//     and with it the SILENT bare-builtin shadow — is vacuous. It CONSUMES no bare name either: every
	//     call in its body is receiver-dot (`source.current()`, `source.advance()`).
	//   • TYPE NAMES: `WithIterIterator` alone. It is declared by no other stdlib file — checked at the
	//     source across all 50 — and by nothing in shv2's own compiler, so the one shape this file calls
	//     SILENT (type-name-vs-builtin) has nothing to contest.
	//   • It declares NO typealias, NO top-level binding and NO enum, so the three clauses that moved
	//     `File.maxon`'s golden, built `S2h`'s reachability term and contested `Ordering` all have nothing
	//     to weigh. That is a declaration COUNT, not a hope.
	//   • E3112 MEMBER names: `create`, `current`, `advance` — the `Iterator` protocol's own vocabulary,
	//     already claimed by every iterator in the library. Its one argument label is `source`.
	//   • It IMPORTS nothing and names only `Iterator`, `IterationError` and its own two parameters, all of
	//     them `Interfaces.maxon`'s, which is listed.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS THE FIRST LISTED MODULE THAT WRITES `implements <Interface> with (A, B)` AT ARITY ONE**, and
	// that spelling is a TUPLE argument rather than two bindings — see `Parser.parenthesizedWithBindsOneTuple`
	// for the rule, which both references already had and shv2's `R6` did not.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE THIRD QUESTION `S2g` ESTABLISHED — what does the entry cost a program that does NOT use it?**
	// MEASURED, same worktree, two binaries, `function main() returns ExitCode / return 7`: **1,592 code
	// bytes both ways — Δ0**, and the two emitted executables are **byte-for-byte identical** (5,567 bytes,
	// `cmp` clean).
	try listWhitelistedModule(stdlibDir, relative: "helpers/itertools/withIterator.maxon", into: listed)

	// ⭐⭐ **W3 — `Interfaces.maxon`, THE PROTOCOL ROOT, AND THE FIRST ENTRY THAT RETIRES A COMPILER-OWNED
	// DECLARATION RATHER THAN MERELY AVOIDING ONE.** Every other module here declares nothing the compiler
	// owns; this one declares `enum Ordering` and `enum IterationError`, both of which the compiler used to
	// TRANSCRIBE (`Project.builtinOrderingEnum`, `Project.builtinIterationErrorEnum`, both now deleted). The
	// declarations arrive through the ordinary path and the compiler reads them
	// (`Project.requireCorpusDeclaredEnum`) — the A1s `__Managed*Error` precedent, and the reason
	// `Parser.requireTypeNameNotCompilerOwned` now admits a stdlib declaration of an owned name.
	//
	// It clears every entry in this file's collision rule, each checked rather than assumed:
	//   • FREE FUNCTIONS: exactly one, `spreadHash`. It is claimed by no other listed module and by no BARE
	//     builtin — checked AT `Parser.parseCallNamed`'s `if` chain, which is what that rule's last bullet
	//     insists on.
	//   • TYPE NAMES: `Error`, `Hashable`, `Equatable`, `Comparable`, `Cloneable`, `Iterator`,
	//     `BidirectionalIterator`, `Iterable`, `Stringable`, `FormattedStringable`, `Ordering`,
	//     `IterationError`. A user program spelling any of the ten INTERFACE names still wins and the stdlib
	//     declaration moves into the reserved space (A1s wave 2); the two ENUM names are compiler-owned, so a
	//     user declaration of either is E2015 and there is nothing to contest.
	//   • ⚠ **THE TEN INTERFACES ARE DECLARED HERE *AND* SYNTHESIZED** (`IrInterface.builtinInterface`), and
	//     that is not a duplicate definition: `lookupInterface` answers from the synthesized copy first, so
	//     the parsed one is inert. It is one fact written twice and it is filed as such — the convergence is
	//     a rung of its own, because the synthesized signatures are what the WITNESS layout is derived from
	//     and moving that is not a whitelist change.
	//   • RANGED typealiases: `HashValue` (`int(0 to u32.max)`) and `IterStep`. `HashValue` is compiler-owned
	//     and erases to `integer` (`TypeResolution.isSynthesizedIntAliasName`), so the declaration mints no
	//     identity and contests nothing; giving it its declared BOUNDS is a separate rung with goldens to
	//     move. `IterStep` is declared by no other listed module.
	//   • ⚠ The E3112 MEMBER-name rule scans this file like every other listed module. Its members are
	//     `hash`, `equals`, `compare`, `clone`, `current`, `advance`, `retreat`, `advanceBy`, `retreatBy`,
	//     `createIterator`, `map`, `filter`, `contains`, `withIterator`, `toString` — ordinary English, which
	//     is exactly what that check protects.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **ITS `extension` BODIES ARE THE CASE THE READINESS CRITERION CANNOT SEE, so this entry was NOT
	// weighed on `E3001` alone.** `maxon-shv2 build stdlib/Interfaces.maxon` reaches `E3001` and nothing else,
	// AND the injection control was run both ways (this file's own headline rule): a
	// `NoSuchType.definitelyUndefined(1)` injected into `advanceBy` — a method of a plain `extension` — IS
	// reported, while the same injection inside `extension Iterable`'s `map` is NOT, because no conformer in
	// that compile forces it to specialize. The extension bodies are therefore driven from a program that
	// declares a conformer and CALLS them (`specs-shv2/interface-extensions.md`), which is what actually
	// compiles them.
	try listWhitelistedModule(stdlibDir, relative: "Interfaces.maxon", into: listed)
	// ⭐⭐ **`stdlib/Sha256.maxon` — `sha256(data ByteArray) returns ByteArray`, FIPS 180-4, PURE MAXON.**
	// No intrinsic, no runtime entry and no target-specific anything: twelve free functions over `Array`,
	// `Vector` and the bitwise operators. It is also the first listed module the COMPILER ITSELF already
	// depends on — `Targets/Macos/MachOWriter.maxon` calls `sha256` for the ad-hoc code-signature hashes and
	// the Mach-O UUID — though it reaches it as a bootstrap-compiled program, which loads all of `stdlib/`
	// and so never consulted this list.
	//
	// ⚠ **ITS BLOCKER WAS REGISTER PRESSURE, NOT A MISSING MECHANISM, AND `S2m-b` REMOVED IT.** The eight
	// round variables plus a 64-word schedule put **23 values live against a pool of 14** (`E5001`); the
	// `Vector`-and-`sha256Compress` rewrite cut the peak to 12, margin 2. So this entry is the whitelist line
	// alone — no compiler change is owed here.
	//
	// It clears every entry in this file's collision rule, each CHECKED rather than assumed:
	//   • FREE FUNCTIONS: **TWELVE**, and the count is RE-DERIVED from the file as it stands rather than
	//     inherited — `S2m-b`'s rewrite minted `sha256Compress` and `sha256AddIntoState`, so an audit taken
	//     before it reports ten. They are `sha256RotR`, `sha256Ch`, `sha256Maj`, `sha256BigSigma0`,
	//     `sha256BigSigma1`, `sha256SmSigma0`, `sha256SmSigma1`, `sha256ReadBE32`, `sha256Compress`,
	//     `sha256AddIntoState`, `sha256` and `emitBE32` — every one but the last `sha256`-prefixed, which is
	//     why `emitBE32` is the one worth naming twice. Checked against `Parser.parseCallNamed`'s bare-name
	//     `if` chain AT THE CHAIN, as that rule's last bullet insists, and in BOTH directions: the chain
	//     claims `trunc floor ceil round sqrt abs min max` (through `calleeTakesPositionalArgsOnly`), `print`,
	//     `runProcess`, `spawnReadLine` and the seven `subp*`, none of which is a name here — and this module
	//     CONSUMES none of them either, the way `Math.pow` consumes `floor`: every call in its body is one of
	//     its own eleven siblings or is receiver-dot / `Type.`-qualified. Against the other listed modules
	//     there is no overlap, checked AT THE SOURCE by reading their declarations rather than a roster
	//     transcribed here — the near miss is `Interfaces.maxon`'s `spreadHash`, the listed set's only other
	//     hashing free function, and it is a miss.
	//   • TYPE NAMES: it declares NO nominal `type`/`enum`/`union`/`interface` AT ALL, so the shadowing
	//     clause (A1s wave 2) and the TYPE-NAME-vs-BUILTIN shape that is still SILENT both have nothing to
	//     weigh. That is a declaration COUNT, not a hope.
	//   • ⚠⚠ **THE RANGED-TYPEALIAS CLAUSE THAT MOVED A GOLDEN FOR `File.maxon` IS LIVE HERE.**
	//     `typealias Word32 = int(i64.min to i64.max)` (`:3`) is file-PRIVATE, which this file's header
	//     measured to be irrelevant: the two whole-program folds that carry it — `widenStorageBytesFor` ⇒
	//     `storageBytesInEveryFile` and `record`'s `multiplyDeclared` ⇒ `settleRangeContests` — key on the
	//     NAME and consult visibility NOWHERE. What decides whether it MOVES anything is whether the compiler
	//     MINTS instances over that element, and the name that does is `Byte`: `Word32` is not it, and no
	//     `b"…"` literal's element can reach this alias. Its FORM is already declared under three other names
	//     by two listed modules (`Builtins.maxon`'s `ParsedInt` and `DecimalPointScale`, `Interfaces.maxon`'s
	//     `IterStep`) — the same same-form-different-name shape `Clock`'s four `int(0 to u64.max)` aliases
	//     have carried since that entry. ⚠ **Checked as a BUILD and not as a reading, because a CONTEST is
	//     what this clause is about**: a program declaring its own NARROWER `typealias Word32 = int(0 to 100)`
	//     and calling nothing here compiles, runs and answers identically under shv2 and the bootstrap.
	//     ⛔⛔ **AND THAT CONTROL AGREED WITH A BUG, WHICH IS WHY IT WAS NOT THE LAST WORD.** `21 * 2` is 42
	//     under either range, so it could not tell "each file reads its own `Word32`" from "one of them won";
	//     this file's own ⛔⛔ paragraph on `sleep` says a control whose two declarations AGREE proves nothing.
	//     Run on declarations that DIFFER — the same narrow `Word32` in a program that also CALLS `sha256`,
	//     whose body holds `0x6a09e667` in one — shv2 **panicked** `sha256: state index unreachable` where the
	//     bootstrap answered `186`. The fault was NOT this module and NOT the whitelist: a range contest
	//     re-interns every generic instance over the contested element per reading file, and that re-intern
	//     dropped the `fixedSize`, so `Vector with 8 Word32` came back SIZELESS. Fixed at
	//     `SignatureIndex.fileScopedInstance`, reproducible with two plain user files and no stdlib at all
	//     (`specs-shv2/typealias-file-scope.md`'s
	//     `a-sized-vector-keeps-its-element-count-when-its-element-is-contested`), and the contest program now
	//     answers `42 186` under both compilers. ⇒ **an entry for a module whose ranged alias is a `Vector`
	//     ELEMENT owes the differing-declarations control, not just the agreeing one.**
	//   • `Sha256Words = Array with Word32` (`:4`) and `Sha256State = Vector with 8 Word32` (`:13`) are
	//     GENERIC-INSTANCE aliases, file-local per alias FORM exactly as `CommandLine`'s `StringArray` is, so
	//     they reserve nothing from a user program and join neither fold.
	//   • ⚠⚠ **THE "TYPE-PARAMETERIZED BY A USER DECLARATION" CLAUSE HAS THE EXACT SHAPE THAT BROKE
	//     `stdlib/URL.maxon` AND THEN `stdlib/CommandLine.maxon`.** `ByteArray` is `File.maxon`'s
	//     `Array with Byte` and it is in this module's own PUBLIC signature (`sha256(data ByteArray) returns
	//     ByteArray`, `:174`) as well as `sha256ReadBE32`'s and `emitBE32`'s — and `Byte` is a name a user may
	//     declare. That clause says checking it is a BUILD and not a reading, so it was built: a program
	//     declaring `typealias Byte = int(0 to 1000)` that calls NOTHING here compiles and runs clean, because
	//     the element the compiler MINTS is `SignatureIndex.SynthesizedByteElementName` rather than a
	//     spelling taken from user-declarable names.
	//   • ⭐⭐ **TOP-LEVEL STATE: `let sha256KTable` (`:16`), THE 64-WORD ROUND-CONSTANT TABLE — AND THIS IS
	//     THE FIRST MODULE OTHER THAN `Log.maxon` TO EXERCISE `S2h`'s CURE.** It is the very shape that cost
	//     `Log.maxon` **+1,386 code bytes (+87%)** on a program that never spelled `Log`, before
	//     `DeadGlobalElimination` gained its reachability term. It is IMMUTABLE and file-PRIVATE, so it is
	//     filed under a key carrying its declaring path (`topLevelStorageKey`) and a user program may still
	//     declare its own `sha256KTable`; an EXPORTED top-level binding would owe the E3006 argument
	//     `Log.maxon`'s entry sets out, and this one does not.
	//   • E3112 fires only when a user program actually contests a spelling, so there is nothing to clear
	//     ahead of time. These are FREE functions with no receiver, so the surface they expose is their
	//     call-site argument LABELS — `n`, `b`, `c`, `off`, `padded`, `blockOff`, `slot`, `addend`, `val`,
	//     `value` — plus the dot-member spellings this file's tokens carry, `.get`/`.set`/`.push`/`.reserve`/
	//     `.append`/`.count`/`.create` and the `i64.min`/`i64.max` bounds, every one of which the listed set
	//     already contests.
	//   • It has NO imports and calls into no other stdlib module.
	//
	// MEASURED before listing, which is the check this file's rule prescribes:
	// `maxon-shv2 build stdlib/Sha256.maxon` exits 1 with `E3001: No 'main' function found` AND NOTHING ELSE.
	// ⚠ And the criterion was PROVED ABLE TO FAIL for these bodies rather than assumed to be evidence — the
	// hole `STDLIB-BRINGUP.md` found is a conditional `extension`, which this module does not contain, but
	// that is an argument and the control is a run: on a copy outside `stdlib/`, verbatim ⇒ `E3001` only;
	// with `let bogus = NoSuchType.definitelyUndefined(1)` injected into `sha256Ch` ⇒ `E3001` **plus `E3004
	// call to undefined function 'NoSuchType.definitelyUndefined'`**.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **THE BYTE A/B — THE MEASUREMENT `Log.maxon` MADE MANDATORY FOR A MODULE WITH TOP-LEVEL STATE, AND
	// THE FIRST TIME `S2h`'s CURE IS CONFIRMED ON A MODULE OTHER THAN `Log`.** MEASURED in ONE tree, two
	// binaries differing only by the line below, on TWO programs rather than one — because "flat" is a claim
	// about the SHAPE and one point cannot carry it:
	//   • `function main() returns ExitCode / return 7` — **1,592 code bytes both ways, Δ0**, and the two
	//     executables are **byte-for-byte identical** (5,567 bytes, `cmp` clean).
	//   • 600 generated functions, 6,004 lines — **48,506 code bytes both ways**, executables again
	//     **byte-for-byte identical**.
	// ⚠ **AND THE `.rdata` WATCH THIS ENTRY OWES, BEING THE FIRST WITH A LARGE CONSTANT TABLE, READS ZERO
	// BECAUSE THE SECTION IS NOT EMITTED AT ALL.** The trivial program's PE carries `.text`/`.idata`/`.rsrc`
	// and NO `.rdata` — `StdlibFacts.unreachable` keeps the 64-word K-table out of the image rather than
	// merely out of the way, which is the same mechanism `Math.maxon`'s 14 float constants measured.
	//
	// ⭐ **WHAT IT DOES COST IS FLAT, AND THAT IS A TWO-POINT MEASUREMENT RATHER THAN AN ARGUMENT** — the
	// per-compile price of one more file lexed, parsed and swept. Allocations rise **+19,147** on the trivial
	// program (301,875 ⇒ 321,022) and **+19,147** on the 6,004-line one (1,110,165 ⇒ 1,129,312): identical to
	// the digit, so nothing here is a term that grows with the user's program. (Bytes: +985,162 and +985,100,
	// the 62-byte gap being the metrics/output path text.) The magnitude is this module's SIZE and not a
	// mechanism — 10,147 source bytes against `Log.maxon`'s 2,293, whose entry measured +3,237 the same way.
	try listWhitelistedModule(stdlibDir, relative: "Sha256.maxon", into: listed)

	// ⭐⭐ **W5 — `stdlib/Process.maxon`, `Process.executablePath()`.** Thirty-three lines: one `static` over
	// one intrinsic. Its blocker was `__Builtins.executablePath`, which did not exist
	// (`E3004 :29:17`, measured at HEAD before this rung); it is now `__proc_exe_path`
	// (`Runtime/ProcessRuntime.maxon`).
	//
	// It clears every entry in this file's collision rule, each CHECKED rather than assumed:
	//   • FREE FUNCTIONS (E3006): it declares NONE — its one function is a `static` of `Process` — so the
	//     clause, and with it the SILENT bare-builtin shadow that cost `Sleep.maxon` a builtin retirement, is
	//     vacuous. Checked against `Parser.parseCallNamed`'s bare-name `if` chain AT THE CHAIN, as that rule's
	//     last bullet insists, and in BOTH directions: it declares nothing the chain can see, and it CONSUMES
	//     nothing the chain claims — every call in its body is `Type.`-qualified (`String.init`,
	//     `FilePath.from`) or `__Builtins.`-prefixed, which is a different namespace.
	//   • TYPE NAMES: `Process` and `ProcessIntrospectionError` are nominal declarations, so a user program
	//     spelling either wins and this module's moves into the reserved space (A1s wave 2, directory-scoped
	//     by N1). Against the other listed modules there is no overlap, checked AT THE SOURCE across all 50
	//     stdlib files rather than against a roster transcribed here: nothing else declares either name.
	//     ⚠ `Subprocess.maxon` is the near miss and it is a miss — it declares `Configuration`, `Environment`,
	//     `InputSource` and their siblings, and it is not listed in any case.
	//   • TYPE-NAME-vs-BUILTIN, the one shape still SILENT: neither is on
	//     `TypeResolution.isCompilerOwnedTypeName` nor `builtinTypeNameTag`, and nothing in the compiler
	//     synthesizes a declaration of either.
	//   • ⚠⚠ **THE RANGED-TYPEALIAS CLAUSE IS LIVE HERE, AND IT LANDS ON `ExitCode` — A NAME THE COMPILER
	//     OWNS AND MINTS INSTANCES OF.** ⛔ **PLAN.md's Wave 1 trap note, and this rung's own contract, BOTH
	//     say `Process.maxon` does not declare `ExitCode`. MEASURED: IT DOES** — `:8` under `#if os(Windows)`
	//     (`int(0 to u32.max)`) and `:15` on the `#else` arm (`int(0 to 255)`), both `export`ed. So this is
	//     the FIRST listed module to declare a compiler-owned type name that is not an enum read from the
	//     corpus, and it is the exact shape that moved `File.maxon`'s golden through `Byte`: `ExitCode` is a
	//     name the compiler mints an instance of in EVERY program (`main() returns ExitCode`).
	//     ⚠ It is legal, and by a rule that predates this rung rather than an exemption bought for it:
	//     `Parser.requireTypeNameNotCompilerOwned` refuses a NOMINAL identity only, and a RANGED alias mints
	//     none — it erases to `integer` exactly as the builtin does, which is why
	//     `specs-shv2/generic-type-nested-array-typealias.md` may declare `typealias ExitCode = int(0 to 125)`
	//     in all 27 of its cases. `denotedNamedType`'s FIRST arm answers `builtinTypeNameTag`'s `exitCode`
	//     before any registry is consulted, so the DENOTATION cannot move.
	//     ⚠ **What that argument does NOT settle is the whole-program RANGE FOLD, so it was MEASURED and not
	//     reasoned:** with this entry present, `--filter=generic-type-nested-array-typealias` — the 27 cases
	//     that declare their own NARROWER `ExitCode` and so are the live contest — is 27/27, and so is the
	//     full suite. The two declarations agree on nothing but the name and the program still answers
	//     correctly, which is the differing-declarations control `Sha256.maxon`'s entry demands.
	//     ⚠⚠ **AND IT MOVES EXACTLY ONE COMMITTED GOLDEN, WHICH IS THE CONTEST BECOMING VISIBLE RATHER THAN
	//     A MEANING CHANGING.** `inner-alias-construction/generic-inner-array-alias` emitted
	//     `__layout_Holder_ExitCode` and now emits `__layout_Holder_ExitCode$0_125`: that spec declares its
	//     own `int(0 to 125)`, so the name is CONTESTED for the first time and each claimant's element takes
	//     its per-claimant suffix — the same `Byte$0_255` mechanism `isByteElementName`'s base-name strip
	//     exists for. A SYMBOL name moved; the case passes and the program's answer is unchanged.
	//     ⭐⭐ **AND IT REMOVES A FALSE REFUSAL, WHICH IS THE POINT OF THE ENTRY RATHER THAN A SIDE EFFECT.**
	//     shv2 knew `ExitCode`'s RANGE (a transcription — BATCH27) while giving the element the machine-word
	//     SLOT of a name no file declares, and `bytearray-element-size`'s raw-byte-write door compared the
	//     two: a program the BOOTSTRAP compiles and runs to 3741319169 was refused `E3118`. Listing the
	//     module makes the slot and the range come from ONE declaration, they agree, and **both compilers now
	//     answer 3741319169** (MEASURED, both ways). The spec case that pinned the refusal is rewritten and
	//     says so.
	//   • The "type-parameterized by a user declaration" clause: its body passes a `__ManagedMemory` to
	//     `String.init` — the EXACT shape that broke `stdlib/URL.maxon` and then `stdlib/CommandLine.maxon`
	//     with `E3005 … expected '__ManagedMemory', got 'Array_Byte'`. That clause says checking it is a BUILD
	//     and not a reading, so it was built: a program declaring `typealias Byte = int(0 to 1000)` that calls
	//     NOTHING here compiles and runs clean, because `emitOwnedManagedMemoryResult` mints the element
	//     through `SignatureIndex.internSynthesizedByteInstance` rather than spelling it out of
	//     user-declarable names.
	//   • It declares NO top-level binding, so it does not inherit the class `S2h` had to build a
	//     reachability term for: `DeadGlobalElimination` has no global of this module's to keep or drop.
	//   • E3112 fires only when a user program actually contests a spelling. The surface is one method name
	//     (`executablePath`) and NO argument label at all — the function takes none.
	//   • It has NO imports; it calls `String.init` and `FilePath.from`, the latter in `FilePath.maxon`, which
	//     is already listed.
	//
	// MEASURED before listing, which is the check this file's rule prescribes: with `__proc_exe_path` built,
	// `maxon-shv2 build stdlib/Process.maxon` exits 1 with `E3001: No 'main' function found` AND NOTHING ELSE.
	try listWhitelistedModule(stdlibDir, relative: "Process.maxon", into: listed)
	// ⭐⭐ **W5 — `stdlib/Console.maxon`, `Console.stdin()` AND THE BUFFERED LINE READER OVER IT.** Its blocker
	// was `__Builtins.readStdin`, which did not exist (`E3004 :62:12`, measured at HEAD before this rung); it
	// is now `__con_read_stdin` (`Runtime/ConsoleRuntime.maxon`).
	//
	// ⭐ **IT IS ON THE 🚩 PHASE 1 GATE's OWN PATH**, which no other entry here is: shv2's extracted spec
	// runner calls `Console.stdin()` at `Testing/SpecWorkerPool.maxon:425` and treats EOF as *"the parent
	// vanished, shut down"*. So the semantics this entry admits are ones the compiler will eventually read
	// about itself, not only ones a user program can.
	//
	// It clears every entry in this file's collision rule, each CHECKED rather than assumed:
	//   • ⚠ FREE FUNCTIONS (E3006): it declares ONE — `stripTrailingCR` (`:178`), file-private. That is worth
	//     naming rather than waving, because `export` is measured to be unreadable at the folds that matter
	//     (this file's header, second ⛔ paragraph). Checked against `Parser.parseCallNamed`'s bare-name `if`
	//     chain AT THE CHAIN, in BOTH directions: the chain claims `print trunc runProcess spawnReadLine` and
	//     the seven `subp*`, plus `floor ceil round sqrt abs min max`, and `stripTrailingCR` is none of them;
	//     nor does this module CONSUME one, the way `Math.pow` consumes `floor` — every call in its body is
	//     receiver-dot or `Type.`-qualified. ⚠ `stdlib/Subprocess.maxon:782` declares a `stripTrailingCR` too,
	//     over a `String` rather than a `ByteArray`, and that file is NOT listed — so there is no
	//     listed-listed collision today and there WILL be one the day it is. Recorded here rather than
	//     discovered there.
	//   • TYPE NAMES: `Console`, `Stdin`, `ConsoleError` and `NotFound` are nominal declarations, so a user
	//     program spelling any of them wins and this module's moves into the reserved space (A1s wave 2,
	//     directory-scoped by N1). Against the other listed modules there is no overlap, checked AT THE SOURCE
	//     across all 50 stdlib files. ⚠ `NotFound` is the one worth stopping on — it is an ordinary English
	//     word and `File.maxon`/`Directory.maxon` both carry a `notFound` CASE — but a case is not a
	//     declaration and neither declares the TYPE.
	//   • TYPE-NAME-vs-BUILTIN, the one shape still SILENT: none of the four is on
	//     `TypeResolution.isCompilerOwnedTypeName` or `builtinTypeNameTag`.
	//   • ⚠⚠ **THE RANGED-TYPEALIAS CLAUSE IS LIVE, AND THIS IS THE FIRST ENTRY WHOSE RANGED ALIAS IS ALREADY
	//     DECLARED BY ANOTHER LISTED MODULE.** `typealias Byte = int(0 to u8.max)` (`:23`) is
	//     `stdlib/File.maxon:45`'s declaration to the byte, and `typealias ByteArray = Array with Byte` (`:24`)
	//     is `:46`'s. That is not the two-listed-modules-declare-one-type-name bug this file warns about,
	//     because neither is NOMINAL: a ranged alias joins `settleRangeContests` by NAME and the two
	//     declarations AGREE, so the contest has nothing to settle, and a generic-instance alias is file-local
	//     per alias FORM. ⚠ Which means the `b"…"` element bounds `File.maxon`'s entry moved a golden for do
	//     not move again — this declaration is the same bounds a second time. **MEASURED rather than argued:
	//     `--filter=byte-string-literal` and `--filter=bytearray-element-size` are green and their goldens are
	//     unmoved.**
	//   • `typealias ByteCount = int(0 to u64.max)` (`:29`) is the one ranged alias genuinely NEW to the listed
	//     set — `stdlib/String.maxon` and `stdlib/TcpClient.maxon` declare the name, and the loader can list
	//     neither. Its FORM is already declared under four other names by `Clock.maxon`, the same
	//     same-form-different-name shape every entry since that one has carried, and the compiler mints no
	//     instance over a `ByteCount` element.
	//   • ⚠⚠⚠ **THE "TYPE-PARAMETERIZED BY A USER DECLARATION" CLAUSE HAS THE EXACT SHAPE THAT BROKE
	//     `stdlib/URL.maxon` AND THEN `stdlib/CommandLine.maxon` — AND HERE IT ACTUALLY FIRED, WHICH IS THE
	//     THIRD TIME AND THE FIRST THAT THE MINTED ELEMENT ALONE DID NOT CURE.** `fillOnce` (`:62-69`)
	//     receives a `__ManagedMemory` from the intrinsic and passes it to `ByteArray.init(mm)`, and the
	//     READINESS PROBE this rule prescribes reported
	//     `E3005 :68:25 argument type mismatch for 'managed': expected '__ManagedMemory with Byte', got
	//     'Array___ManagedByte'`. The producer side was already right — the intrinsic mints
	//     `SignatureIndex.SynthesizedByteElementName`, exactly as this clause demands. The CONSUMER was not:
	//     `Parser.requireSameArrayInstance` asked raw instance EQUALITY, so it refused the compiler's own
	//     mint at a declared byte position. That is the NINTH door of the class
	//     `ProgramSignatures.byteBufferBoundaryAdmits`'s header enumerates — and the only one that does not
	//     ride `aggregatesConflict`, which is exactly why the other eight did not cover it — and it now asks that shared
	//     three-question door like the other seven. ⇒ **The clause's cure is TWO-SIDED and the loader header
	//     only ever stated one side**: minting a compiler-owned element is necessary, and every door that
	//     receives one must know the boundary. `specs-shv2/array-init.md`'s two new cases are the pins.
	//     ⚠ The rest of the clause was checked as a BUILD, which is what it demands: a program declaring
	//     `typealias Byte = int(0 to 1000)` that calls NOTHING here compiles and runs clean.
	//   • ⭐ **TOP-LEVEL BINDING: `let chunkSize = 4096` (`:33`) — THE `S2h` SHAPE, AND ITS THIRD
	//     CONFIRMATION.** It is IMMUTABLE and file-PRIVATE, so it is filed under a key carrying its declaring
	//     path (`topLevelStorageKey`) and a user program may still declare its own `chunkSize`; an EXPORTED
	//     top-level binding would owe the E3006 argument `Log.maxon`'s entry sets out, and this one does not.
	//     The byte A/B this shape makes mandatory is recorded at the foot of this entry.
	//   • E3112 fires only when a user program actually contests a spelling. The surface is the method names
	//     (`stdin`, `create`, `eof`, `fillOnce`, `readLine`, `readBytes`, `findNewline`, `takePrefix`,
	//     `discardOne`, `shiftLeft`), two fields (`pending`, `eofSeen`) and the labels `n`/`bytes`. Counted
	//     against what the listed set ALREADY contests rather than against this module's own roster: `create`
	//     is `FileInfo.create`'s and `n` is `Sha256.maxon`'s, so the spellings genuinely NEW are the eight
	//     reader methods plus `pending`, `eofSeen` and `bytes`.
	//   • It has NO imports and calls into no other stdlib module.
	//
	// MEASURED as the check this file's rule prescribes — and it FAILED first, which is the whole value of
	// running it: with `__con_read_stdin` built but the byte-buffer door unfixed it reported the `E3005`
	// above, and with both in place `maxon-shv2 build stdlib/Console.maxon` exits 1 with
	// `E3001: No 'main' function found` AND NOTHING ELSE.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **THE BYTE A/B — MANDATORY FOR A MODULE WITH TOP-LEVEL STATE SINCE `Log.maxon`, AND THIS IS THE
	// THIRD CONFIRMATION OF `S2h`'s CURE (after `Log` itself and `Sha256.maxon`).** MEASURED in ONE tree, two
	// binaries differing ONLY by the two `listWhitelistedModule` lines above, on TWO programs rather than one
	// — because "flat" is a claim about the SHAPE and one point cannot carry it:
	//   • `function main() returns ExitCode / return 7` — **1,592 code bytes both ways, Δ0**, and the two
	//     executables are **byte-for-byte identical** (5,567 bytes, `cmp` clean).
	//   • 600 generated functions, 7,204 lines — **68,334 code bytes both ways**, executables again
	//     **byte-for-byte identical** (82,927 bytes, `cmp` clean).
	//
	// ⭐ **WHAT IT DOES COST IS FLAT IN THE USER'S PROGRAM, AND THAT IS THE TWO-POINT MEASUREMENT AND NOT AN
	// ARGUMENT** — the per-compile price of two more files lexed, parsed and swept. Allocations rise
	// **+11,698** on the trivial program (321,911 ⇒ 333,609) and **+11,699** on the 7,204-line one
	// (1,402,973 ⇒ 1,414,672): identical to within one allocation of output-path text, so nothing here is a
	// term that grows with the user's program.
	// ⚠ **BYTES ARE NOT FLAT AND THE COUNT IS — +586,397 against +1,744,537 — WHICH IS A READING TO EXPLAIN
	// RATHER THAN A COST TO ACCEPT SILENTLY.** Attributed by the per-phase table: **`merge` carries
	// +1,205,048 of the large program's delta for only +1,043 allocations**, i.e. the same allocations at a
	// bigger size. Two more files' declarations push the merged module's growable arrays across a DOUBLING
	// they would otherwise have reached later, and a doubling reallocates in proportion to what is already
	// there. That predicts a constant STEP on the scale ladder rather than a bend, which is the reading the
	// ladder can confirm and this A/B cannot.
	try listWhitelistedModule(stdlibDir, relative: "Console.maxon", into: listed)
	// ⭐⭐ **W35 — `stdlib/Print.maxon` AND `stdlib/PrintError.maxon`, AND THIS IS THE THIRD APPLICATION OF
	// THE BARE-BUILTIN RULE ABOVE.** Eleven lines and six between them: one `export function` each, over one
	// intrinsic. Their blockers were `__Builtins.writeStdout`/`writeStderr`, which did not exist — MEASURED
	// at HEAD before this rung, `E3004 stdlib\PrintError.maxon:5:2` — and, for `Print.maxon` alone, a
	// bare-name `print` BUILTIN that claimed the call before any registry was consulted.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **THE BUILTIN IS THE ENTRY, NOT A FOOTNOTE, AND THE ROW THAT FILED THIS RUNG GOT THE HARM WRONG.**
	// It predicted an `E3006` collision. MEASURED with both entries added and the builtin still live:
	// `print("hi\n")` compiled CLEAN — 1,703 bytes, no diagnostic — while `printError(…)` one file over
	// raised the `E3004` above, which proves `PrintError.maxon` WAS analyzed and `Print.maxon` was NEVER
	// REACHED. So the harm was not a refusal: it was a NO-OP entry that counts +1 on the cone and changes
	// nothing, which is this project's own recurring class. The rule's conclusion survives its wrong
	// premise, because the rule is about REACHABILITY and not about diagnostics. The builtin is retired
	// (see `Parser.maxon`, where `PrintBuiltinName` was declared).
	//
	// ⭐ **AND THE DEPENDENCY RUNS THE OTHER WAY TOO: `stdlib/Build.maxon` — ALREADY LISTED — CALLS `print`
	// TWELVE TIMES.** While the builtin stood, the chain answered those calls; the moment it is retired they
	// need a declaration, so `Print.maxon` is not merely unblocked by the retirement, it is REQUIRED by it.
	// That is the pair of edits this entry and the builtin's deletion have to land in one commit.
	//
	// They clear every entry in this file's collision rule, each CHECKED rather than assumed. Most clauses
	// are vacuous here, and saying WHY is cheaper than leaving a reader to wonder which were skipped:
	//   • FREE FUNCTIONS: each declares exactly ONE, `print` and `printError`, and nothing else at all — no
	//     type, no typealias, no top-level binding. Checked against `Parser.parseCallNamed`'s bare-name `if`
	//     chain AT THE CHAIN, as that rule's last bullet insists, and in BOTH directions. INWARD: after this
	//     rung the chain claims `runProcess spawnReadLine` + the seven `subp*` + `trunc floor ceil round sqrt
	//     abs min max`, and neither name is among them — `print` was, which is the whole first half of this
	//     entry. OUTWARD: `Print.maxon`'s body consumes NO bare name (`value.addressableBytes()` is
	//     receiver-dot, `__Builtins.writeStdout` is prefix-qualified), and neither does `PrintError.maxon`'s.
	//   • ⚠ THE SIXTH SURFACE — the LISTED SET's OWN free functions (W5 review) — is the one clause that can
	//     bite a module declaring only free functions, so it was checked at the source across all 50 stdlib
	//     files rather than against a roster: `print` and `printError` are declared by these two files and by
	//     NOTHING else in `stdlib/`, listed or not. There is no `stripTrailingCR`-shaped collision waiting.
	//   • TYPE NAMES (user shadowing, listed-vs-listed, and the still-SILENT builtin case): all three are
	//     vacuous — neither module declares a type at all.
	//   • The RANGED-TYPEALIAS clause, the one that moved `File.maxon`'s golden through `Byte` and that
	//     `Process.maxon` met through `ExitCode`: vacuous, neither declares a `typealias`.
	//   • E3112 fires only when a user program actually contests a spelling. The surface is ONE parameter
	//     name, `value`, shared by both — and it is a FIRST parameter, so no call site ever spells it as a
	//     label. It is contested by the listed set already in any case (`Sha256.maxon`'s `value:`).
	//   • ⚠ THE "TYPE-PARAMETERIZED BY A USER DECLARATION" CLAUSE IS LIVE, and it is the one that broke
	//     `URL.maxon`, then `CommandLine.maxon`, then `Console.maxon`. `addressableBytes()` hands the
	//     intrinsic a byte buffer, which is exactly the `__ManagedMemory`/`Byte` shape those three met. It is
	//     clear on BOTH sides: the PRODUCER mints `SignatureIndex.SynthesizedByteElementName` (the compiler's
	//     own element, not one spelled out of user-declarable names), and the CONSUMER is
	//     `Parser.valueIsByteArray` — the shared byte-packed-buffer predicate, which admits that mint. The
	//     clause says checking it is a BUILD and not a reading, so it was built: a program declaring
	//     `typealias Byte = int(0 to 1000)` that calls neither module compiles and runs clean, and one that
	//     calls `print` does too.
	//   • NO top-level binding, so the `S2h` reachability term has nothing of theirs to keep or drop — and
	//     the byte A/B this file makes mandatory for a module with state is therefore not owed. It was run
	//     anyway, because these two are reached by far more of the corpus than any previous entry.
	//   • NO imports. Their only outward reference is the intrinsic, which is the compiler.
	//
	// MEASURED before listing, which is the check this file's rule prescribes: with both intrinsics built,
	// `maxon-shv2 build stdlib/Print.maxon` and `… stdlib/PrintError.maxon` each exit 1 with
	// `E3001: No 'main' function found` AND NOTHING ELSE.
	try listWhitelistedModule(stdlibDir, relative: "Print.maxon", into: listed)
	try listWhitelistedModule(stdlibDir, relative: "PrintError.maxon", into: listed)
	// ⭐⭐⭐ **W17 — `String`, `Character` AND THE THREE VIEWS: THE CONVERGENCE ENTRY, AND THE FIRST LISTED
	// MODULES THAT DECLARE A COMPILER-OWNED TYPE NAME.** Every entry above this one adds declarations the
	// compiler did not already have. These three REPLACE the authority for something it has always had: the
	// 56-byte byte record. `type String`'s two fields and `type Character`'s one now LAY OUT that record
	// (`Project.StructLayout.isInlineManagedField` — the envelope collapse), instead of `StringRuntime`'s six
	// offsets being the only statement of it.
	//
	// ⚠ **THEY ARE ONE CONE IN THREE LINES, like `URL`/`urlHelpers` above and for the same reason:** none of
	// the three compiles alone. `Character` names `ByteView` (measured: `E3011 Unknown type 'ByteView'` at the
	// probe, once its own blockers were cleared), `String` names `Character` (`E2015 … 'toString'`), and the
	// views name both. Order-independent like every other entry, since `queryProgramSignatures` folds every
	// file's declarations before any file parses.
	//
	// ⭐⭐⭐ **AND THE SECOND HALF OF THAT REPLACEMENT IS THE CONFORMANCE:** the corpus's own
	// `hash`/`equals`/`compare`/`clone`/`toString` are now the bodies a `__witness_String.*` slot relocates
	// to, where `BuiltinConformanceRuntime` used to synthesize every one of them
	// (`undefinedImplNames` builds an impl only for a name the module does not already define).
	//
	// **THE COLLISION RULE, CLEARED PER MODULE** (the checks this file prescribes, run rather than assumed):
	//   • `helpers/string/views.maxon` — no compiler-owned type name (`ByteView`, `CodepointView`,
	//     `UTF16View` and their three iterators are the corpus's own), no free function contested by a bare
	//     builtin or by another listed module, no top-level binding, and its only outward references are
	//     `String` and `Character` — which is why it is one cone with them and not an entry of its own.
	//   • `Character.maxon` — `Character` IS compiler-owned, which is the point: `recordStruct`'s provenance
	//     bit (W16) admits it from the CORPUS and drops it from a user file, so a user declaration is still
	//     E2015. Its free functions are none; its inner `typealias ByteMemory` is type-scoped and therefore
	//     excluded from the DISPLAY index (`deriveInstanceDisplayNames`), so no diagnostic can quote a
	//     spelling the author cannot write.
	//   • `String.maxon` — the same provenance admission for `String`. Its four free functions (`bytesEqual`,
	//     `findIn`, `findLastIn`, `scanSingleByteGraphemes`, `scanIsAscii`) are claimed by no bare builtin in
	//     `Parser.parseCallNamed`'s chain and by no other listed module. `StringIndex`/`StringIterator` are
	//     declared BY this file, and `StringIndex`'s declaration is the ONLY one as of W49 wave 3: the
	//     compiler held a second layout under the reserved spelling `__StringIndex` until then, so what read
	//     "the corpus's bare-named one lands in a different bucket" is now ONE bucket with a reservation over
	//     WHOSE declaration may fill it (`SignatureIndex.StringIndexTypeName`,
	//     `TypeResolution.isCompilerOwnedTypeName`) — the same arrangement `String` and `Character` are
	//     admitted under two bullets up.
	//   • NO top-level binding in any of the three, so the `S2h` reachability term has nothing of theirs to
	//     keep or drop.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **THE WALL SEQUENCE IS THE RECORD OF WHAT IT TOOK, AND IT IS KEPT BECAUSE IT IS THE ONLY THING THAT
	// SAYS WHAT KIND OF WORK THIS WAS.** Each was measured by the same one-command probe this file's own
	// collision rule prescribes — `maxon-shv2 build stdlib/String.maxon` — and each is a place where the
	// CORPUS's calling style met shv2's rule, not a missing feature:
	//   • `:115` the buffer surface's arg-list protocol, · `:447` the roster-wins order for a corpus method,
	//   • `:542` `selfBytes.slice(pos, matchPos)` — CLEARED by routing an implicit-`self`
	//     `addressableBytes()` through the roster arm that marks the buffer surface
	//     (`Parser.implicitSelfTakesTheByteRecordRoster`); the corpus writes the second argument positionally.
	//   • `:658` `work.setByte(i, b + delta)` — CLEARED by giving `String` the buffer's arg-list protocol for
	//     `setByte`, because the corpus writes it POSITIONALLY where shv2 requires named arguments after the
	//     first (E2053).
	//   • `:830` `bytes.appendMemory(piece)` — CLEARED by giving the `Array` surface the member the CORPUS
	//     declares on it (`stdlib/Array.maxon:272`); the roster IS the refusal.
	//   • `:861` `String.fromOwnedBytes(…)` — CLEARED by building the STATIC half of the corpus fall-through:
	//     the compiler's `String` static roster answered ahead of this very file's own declaration. (That
	//     fall-through was `Parser.stringStaticBelongsToTheCorpus`; W55 retired the roster it guarded, so the
	//     ordinary qualified-call path serves every `String` static now and there is no half left to be.)
	//   ⇒ and with `:861` the module PARSES TO ITS END, which is the bar this file sets for listing
	//   ("a module must parse to its END before it is listed, not merely further than it did yesterday").
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **KEEP THIS: THE ENTRY CARRIED THREE FALSE DIAGNOSES, AND EVERY ONE WAS REASONED FROM THE SHAPE OF
	// A FAILURE RATHER THAN FROM THE DECLARATION OR THE SECTION.** It is the most reusable thing here.
	//   1. `:830` was called a `__ManagedMemory` member. It is declared on `Array` and on nothing else, in
	//      the corpus or in either reference.
	//   2. `:830` was then called a surface-propagation gap, with `bytes` declared `ByteBuffer` — a spelling
	//      that has never existed in that file (`git log --all -S "bytes as ByteBuffer"` finds no commit).
	//      The receiver is `var bytes as ByteArray` (`:796`). ⭐ The MECHANISM that diagnosis motivated is
	//      real and stays (`ProgramSignatures.derivedAliasSurfaceBits`, a buffer ALIAS at a struct field /
	//      declared return / union payload swept before the alias was registered) — it simply was never what
	//      `:830` needed.
	//   3. The five `static-variables/data-section-*` failures were called a `let`-with-constant-initializer
	//      storage question — *"both are top-level `let`s … and need no `.data` slot at all; giving them none
	//      is the fix"*. shv2 ALREADY gives a scalar `let` no slot, so the proposed cure was a NO-OP. The
	//      first `--emit-ir` said so: the 24 bytes were `MmRuntime.runtimeGlobals()`, the compiler's OWN
	//      allocator slots, laid down under `usage.usesHeap` for a program whose whole text is `return 0`.
	//      ⇒ It was a RUNTIME-FLOOR regression breaking `Compiler.compileToCodeResult`'s own invariant, and
	//      `RequiredData`'s prefix compare was the instrument that CAUGHT it, not the thing at fault.
	//      ⚖ USER RULING: fix the floor, do not weaken `RequiredData` — re-pinning would bake the compiler's
	//      private slots into a spec about a user's globals and retire a real property.
	//   ⇒ **The pattern: a refusal names the surface it was ASKED about, and a section names the bytes that
	//   are IN it. Neither names the declaration that caused them. Read the declaration.**
	//
	// ⭐ **THE FLOOR REPAIR ITSELF WAS TWO INDEPENDENT OVER-APPROXIMATIONS, EACH SUFFICIENT ALONE**, and both
	// are narrowed rather than disabled — see `StdlibSource.functionDeclaresAWitnessPosition` (the corpus
	// intrinsic conformance seed now fires only where a witness table can actually be materialized) and
	// `MmRuntime.noteDestructorUsage` (the pre-pipeline PREDICTION of which layout-descriptor relocs lowering
	// would stamp is deleted in favour of `noteRelocatedFunctionUsage`, which reads the stamps). With both,
	// `maxon-shv2 build --emit-ir` on `return 0 as ExitCode` emits an EMPTY data section under this listing.
	//
	// ⚠ **TWO SPEC EXPECTATIONS MOVED WITH THE LISTING, AND ONLY ONE OF THE TWO THAT WERE PREDICTED.**
	// `builtin-member-rosters.error.unknown-character-method-gets-the-roster` had to re-point from
	// `codepoint` to `isUpperCase`, because `stdlib/Character.maxon:123` DECLARES `codepoint()` and the
	// corpus fall-through now serves it — the listing working. The predicted second move did NOT come due:
	// `reserved-double-underscore.error.stdlib-overlay-mm-leak-check-takes-a-rooted-name` still takes the
	// A1w arm, because with the floor repaired a heap-free program installs no `__mm_leak_check` after all —
	// which is that case doubling as a second witness for the repair.
	try listWhitelistedModule(stdlibDir, relative: "helpers/string/views.maxon", into: listed)
	try listWhitelistedModule(stdlibDir, relative: "Character.maxon", into: listed)
	try listWhitelistedModule(stdlibDir, relative: "String.maxon", into: listed)
	// ⭐⭐ **`stdlib/Map.maxon` — AND IT RETIRED THE SYNTHESIZED `Map`, WHICH IS WHY THE TWO WERE ONE CHANGE.**
	// Listing it while the builtin still shadowed would have loaded a `type Map` no call site could reach —
	// `Sleep.maxon`'s defect exactly, and this file's own rule names it. W41 added the shared retirement
	// switch so a DECLARED base won every query; W105 then deleted the synthesized regime outright, so this
	// line is no longer merely what makes the corpus type the real one — it is what makes `Map` EXIST.
	// Its slot vocabulary is the shared module's above (W93).
	// ⭐⭐ **`stdlib/helpers/hashtable/slotScan.maxon` — THE SLOT VOCABULARY `Map` AND `Set` BOTH DECLARED,
	// NOW DECLARED ONCE (W93).** It is listed BEFORE its two consumers because it is their dependency, not
	// because load order decides anything: name resolution is whole-program, and this list is a SET.
	//
	// ⭐ **WHAT IT CURES IS AN ASYMMETRIC FAILURE, WHICH IS WHY "they were both correct" WAS NOT ENOUGH.**
	// `Map.maxon` held `enum SlotState { Empty, Occupied, Deleted }` and `Set.maxon` held `SetSlotState` with
	// the same three cases; each had a `findNextOccupied` scan over its own. Add a state to ONE and you get a
	// compile error at THAT file's `match`es and **SILENCE at the other's** — a wrong iteration answer from a
	// container that still compiles. One enum makes every `match` over a slot state, in both files, refuse the
	// new case at once; SABOTAGE-CHECKED by adding a fourth case and watching the one shared body fail.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE COLLISION RULE, CLEARED — each entry CHECKED against the tree rather than against this list:**
	//   • its one FREE function is `findNextOccupied`, which `Parser.parseCallNamed`'s bare-name chain does
	//     not claim (checked at the chain, never at a prose roster) and which no other listed module declares
	//     — `Map.maxon`'s private copy of that very name is DELETED by this change, not shadowed by it;
	//   • its TYPE names are `SlotState`, `SlotStateArray`, `TableSlotIndex` and `TableCapacity`, none
	//     declared by any other listed module, none a builtin, and none spelled anywhere else in the tree;
	//   • ⚠ **THE OLD BARE SPELLINGS COULD NOT BE KEPT, AND THAT IS THE CHECK THIS ENTRY ALMOST MISSED.**
	//     `Map`/`Set` called them `SlotIndex` and `BucketCapacity` while both were FILE-PRIVATE. Exported they
	//     are visible to every program the compiler builds — including the COMPILER's own sources, since the
	//     bootstrap loads all of `stdlib/` — and `SlotIndex` is a local binding in
	//     `maxon-shv2/Compiler/Targets/X64/X64Runtime.maxon`. Hence `Table…`;
	//   • it CONSUMES `IterationError` (`stdlib/Interfaces.maxon:28`) and `Array`, both listed above, so it is
	//     a second reader of two declarations and not a second declaration of either;
	//   • it has no top-level binding, so the `S2h` reachability term has nothing of its to keep or drop, and
	//     it is not type-parameterized by anything a user program can declare — its one generic is
	//     `Array with SlotState`, over its own enum.
	//
	// ⚠ **AND IT IS NOT INERT, WHICH IS THE THIRD `STDLIB-BRINGUP` QUESTION AND THE ONE `W63`/`BATCH36` FAILED
	// ON.** A listed module whose type the compiler SYNTHESIZES builds green and does nothing. Nothing here is
	// synthesized — `SlotState` has no builtin twin and `findNextOccupied` no runtime one — and the injection
	// control confirms it rather than the reading: an undefined call planted in this body is reported against
	// THIS FILE, and both containers' iteration goes red without it.
	try listWhitelistedModule(stdlibDir, relative: "helpers/hashtable/slotScan.maxon", into: listed)
	try listWhitelistedModule(stdlibDir, relative: "Map.maxon", into: listed)
	// ⭐⭐ **`stdlib/Set.maxon` — THE HASH TABLE'S OTHER HALF, AND THE SAME ONE CHANGE (W90).** Everything the
	// entry above says applies here word for word, through what was then a SHARED retirement switch
	// (`isUnretiredBuiltinBaseName`, DELETED at W105 with the regime its true arm selected): listing the
	// module while the builtin still shadowed
	// buys NOTHING — MEASURED, with `count()` rewritten to `return count + 100` the program still answered
	// **2** and the executable was byte-identical — because every `Set` query routed through the switch.
	// The listing plus the switch was the retirement, and the same sabotage then answered **102**. W105 deleted
	// the switch and the `__set_*` runtime behind it, so this line is now what makes `Set` EXIST.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT CARRIES ONE OBLIGATION `Map` DID NOT, AND IT IS WHY THIS ENTRY WAS NOT TAKEN WITH `Map`'s.**
	// `CharacterSet` OWNS a `Set with Character` and its three synthesized `__cs_*` bodies BUILD, DROP and
	// PROBE it. Those callees are not the Maxon call graph's — the caller does not exist when the reachability
	// walk runs — so all three had to be re-pointed at the corpus type by hand and rooted by hand: the build,
	// the drop (a per-instance `__destruct_Set_Character`), the probe (which also took a threaded LAYOUT
	// DESCRIPTOR), plus two reachability seeds for the edges nothing could see. Get any of
	// them wrong and there is no diagnostic — the backend panics in `resolveCallFixups`, or the callee's
	// `param` ops read whatever the ABI registers held. **That obligation is DISCHARGED and its machinery
	// deleted (W129): the corpus declares `CharacterSet`, so no synthesized body owns a `Set` any more.**
	//
	// ⚠ **THE COLLISION RULE, CLEARED:** it declares NO free function since W93 moved `findNextOccupiedSet`
	// into `helpers/hashtable/slotScan.maxon` as the shared `findNextOccupied`. It CONSUMES
	// `spreadHash`, which is `stdlib/Interfaces.maxon:105`'s — already listed above and already consumed the
	// same way by `Map.maxon`, so this is a second reader of one declaration and not a second declaration. Its
	// TYPE names are `Set` (compiler-owned, admitted from the CORPUS by `recordStruct`'s provenance bit exactly
	// as `Map`/`Array`/`String` are) and `SetIterator`, which no other module declares; and it has no
	// top-level binding, so the `S2h` reachability term has nothing of its to keep or drop.
	try listWhitelistedModule(stdlibDir, relative: "Set.maxon", into: listed)
	// ⭐⭐ **`stdlib/CharacterSet.maxon` PLUS `stdlib/helpers/string/unicodeCategory.maxon` — THE OBLIGATION
	// THE ENTRY ABOVE NAMED, DISCHARGED (W115).** `Set.maxon`'s entry records that `CharacterSet` OWNS a
	// `Set with Character` and that three synthesized `__cs_*` bodies build, drop and probe it. The corpus
	// declares the same eleven presets, the same `from` and the same `contains` in 137 lines of Maxon, and
	// with the parser's `CharacterSet` interceptions gone (`parseQualifiedCall`, `dispatchStructMethod`,
	// `parseTypeReference`) every one of them is now an ordinary declared static on an ordinary declared
	// struct.
	//
	// ✅ **THE `__cs_*` RUNTIME IS DELETED (W129)** — `W90`/`W105`'s split, deliberately repeated and
	// discharged. `W90` retired `Set` and left `SetRuntime.maxon` standing; `W105` deleted it separately and
	// its acceptance was BYTE-IDENTITY rather than a green suite, because a green suite says nothing broke
	// while only byte-identity says nothing was LIVE. Deleting 500 lines in the rung that changed behaviour
	// would have made both unattributable.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS ONE CONE IN TWO LINES, AND THE SECOND IS NOT OPTIONAL.** `CharacterSet.contains` classifies a
	// codepoint through `unicodeGeneralCategory` (`stdlib/CharacterSet.maxon:129`), which only
	// `helpers/string/unicodeCategory.maxon` declares — listing the type alone is **`E3004 call to undefined
	// function 'unicodeGeneralCategory'`**, measured. ⇒ **that module's standing *"inert if listed"*
	// classification is REFUTED**: it was inert only while nothing the compiler compiled could call it, and
	// this line is what gives it its caller. Order-independent like every other entry.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE COLLISION RULE, CLEARED — each claim checked against the tree rather than against this list:**
	//   • the cone's one FREE function is `unicodeGeneralCategory`, declared nowhere else in `stdlib/` and not
	//     claimed by `Parser.parseCallNamed`'s bare-name chain;
	//   • its TYPE names are `CharacterSet` and `CharSet` — both compiler-owned, and admitted from the CORPUS
	//     by `recordStruct`'s provenance bit exactly as `Set`/`Map`/`String` are — plus the ranged aliases
	//     `CategoryBitmask` and `GeneralCategory`, which no other module declares. ⚠ **THOSE TWO ARE NOT
	//     `export`ed AND ARE STILL PROGRAM-VISIBLE, so "unexported" is not the reason they are safe** (found
	//     at review, MEASURED: a program that declares neither compiles `let m = 5 as CategoryBitmask` and
	//     prints `5`). What makes them safe is the clause actually checked — nothing else declares them — and
	//     a program that DOES declare its own keeps its own (measured both ways: `int(0 to 5)` refuses 100 in
	//     the user's file while `stdlib/CharacterSet.maxon`'s own `58720256` masks still compile beside it);
	//   • it CONSUMES `Set`, `Character` and `Codepoint`, all listed or builtin above, so it is a second
	//     reader of three declarations and not a second declaration of any;
	//   • `unicodeCategory.maxon`'s forty top-level `let`s are FILE-PRIVATE scalars and only `UCD_SUPP_COUNT`
	//     has a reader, so the `S2h` reachability term drops the rest exactly as it drops any unread global.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **AND IT IS NOT INERT — THE DIFFERING-DECLARATIONS CONTROL PASSES, WHICH `W63`/`W86`/`W112` COULD
	// NOT SAY.** Measured on this tree, one module sabotaged at a time: `contains`'s final `return false` →
	// `return true` moves `[hello] true false` to `[] true true`; `unicodeGeneralCategory`'s BMP arm →
	// `return 0` moves it to `[  hello  ] false false`; and an undefined call planted in that body is
	// ANALYZED and blamed on THIS FILE. Against the unlisted tree all three left the answer — and the
	// executable's byte count — unmoved, which is what a listed-but-inert module looks like.
	try listWhitelistedModule(stdlibDir, relative: "helpers/string/unicodeCategory.maxon", into: listed)
	try listWhitelistedModule(stdlibDir, relative: "CharacterSet.maxon", into: listed)
	// ⭐⭐⭐ **`stdlib/Array.maxon` AND ITS SIX SORT HELPERS — THE ENTRY THIS WHOLE LIST WAS BUILT TOWARD, AND
	// THE LARGEST ONE IT WILL EVER TAKE.** Every entry above adds a capability or replaces an authority for
	// something peripheral. This one replaces the authority for the container the COMPILER ITSELF is built
	// out of: `Array` stops being a set of hand-built `StdOp` graphs and becomes 664 lines of compiled Maxon,
	// against which every one of the suite's 5,100+ cases is now compiled. `String`/`Character` (W17) moved
	// the byte record; this moves the element buffer, the growth policy, the iterator and the copy semantics
	// at once.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS ONE CONE IN SEVEN LINES, and the six helpers are not optional extras.** `Array.sort()` and
	// `Array.sort(cmp)` are declared in `stdlib/Array.maxon:601` and `:621` and immediately hand off to
	// `driftsort`/`pdqsort`, which call `smallSort`, `insertionSort`, `mergeSort` and `driftQuicksort`, and
	// every one of those files is an `export extension Array` — so listing `Array.maxon` alone would load a
	// `sort` whose body is undefined. Order-independent like every other entry, since `queryProgramSignatures`
	// folds every file's declarations before any file parses. All six also call `Log.trace`
	// (`driftsort.maxon:154`), which is why this entry DEPENDS on `Log.maxon` being listed above it — the
	// second entry to consume another listed module's declarations, after `Build.maxon`'s twelve `print`s.
	//
	// **THE COLLISION RULE, CLEARED ACROSS ALL SEVEN FILES, each CHECKED rather than assumed:**
	//   • FREE FUNCTIONS (E3006): **TWO**, and both are `stdlib/Array.maxon`'s — `grownCapacity` (`:65`) and
	//     `atLeastMinimum` (`:97`), the growth-policy arithmetic. Both are file-PRIVATE. ⚠ The six sort
	//     helpers declare NONE AT ALL: every function in them is a member of an `export extension Array`,
	//     which is why the substantial-looking 1,507 lines of sorting add nothing to the free-function space.
	//     Checked against `Parser.parseCallNamed`'s bare-name `if` chain AT THE CHAIN, as that rule's last
	//     bullet insists: it claims `runProcess`, `spawnReadLine`, the seven `subp*`, and the math roster
	//     reached through `calleeTakesPositionalArgsOnly` (`trunc floor ceil round sqrt abs min max`) —
	//     neither name is among them. Against the other listed modules there is no overlap either, checked
	//     AT THE SOURCE across all 50 stdlib files: `grownCapacity` occurs in exactly one other place in
	//     `stdlib/`, `stdlib/String.maxon:782`, and it is a COMMENT pointing here — a reference, not a
	//     declaration, and the distinction is the whole bullet.
	//   • ⚠ These files also CONSUME nothing the chain claims: every call in them is receiver-dot,
	//     `Type.`-qualified (`Log.trace`), or one of their own siblings.
	//   • ⚠⚠ **TYPE NAMES — AND `Array` IS COMPILER-OWNED, which makes this the third entry ever to declare
	//     such a name and the first to do it for a GENERIC base.** `String` and `Character` (W17) are the
	//     precedent and the mechanism is theirs unchanged: `ProgramSignatures.recordStruct`'s provenance bit
	//     (W16) admits the declaration from the CORPUS and drops it from a user file, so a user
	//     `type Array` is still E2015 and `array-slots.error.declare-array-error-enum` still passes.
	//     `ArrayIterator` (`:490`) and `driftsort.maxon`'s `SortRun` (`:57`) are ordinary nominal
	//     declarations — checked AT THE SOURCE across all 50 files, nothing else declares either — so a user
	//     program spelling one wins and this module's moves into the reserved space (A1s wave 2).
	//   • ⚠ **RANGED typealiases: FOUR, and the clause that moved `File.maxon`'s golden is VACUOUS for all
	//     of them, which is a spelling check rather than a hope.** `ElementCount` and `ElementIndex` (`:11`,
	//     `:12`) and `driftsort.maxon`'s `SortRunIndex` (`:39`) are all `int(0 to u64.max)`; `SortRunPower`
	//     (`:40`) is `int(0 to 64)`. What decides whether a ranged alias MOVES anything is whether the
	//     compiler mints instances over that element, and the name that does is `Byte`: none of the four is
	//     spelled `Byte`, so no `b"…"` literal's element can reach them. The three sharing
	//     `int(0 to u64.max)` carry the same same-form-different-name shape `Clock`'s four aliases have had
	//     since that entry. `Array.maxon` declares one further alias INSIDE the type (`ArrayIter`, `:471`),
	//     which is type-scoped and therefore excluded from the display index exactly as `Character`'s
	//     `ByteMemory` is.
	//   • ⭐ **TOP-LEVEL BINDINGS: `GrowthThreshold` (`:56`) and `MinimumCapacity` (`:60`)** — the third
	//     module after `Log.maxon` and `Sha256.maxon` to exercise `S2h`'s reachability term. Both are
	//     IMMUTABLE, file-PRIVATE and SCALAR, so each is filed under a key carrying its declaring path
	//     (`topLevelStorageKey`) and a user program may still declare its own; an EXPORTED one would owe the
	//     E3006 argument `Log.maxon`'s entry sets out, and these do not.
	//   • The E3112 MEMBER-name rule is a whole-program CHECK that fires only when a user program actually
	//     contests a spelling. What it protects is at its most real here — this is the most ordinary-English
	//     surface the listed set has ever taken on (`get`, `set`, `first`, `last`, `pop`, `remove`, `slice`,
	//     `count`, `push`, `clear`, `insert`, `append`, `map`, `contains`, `sort`) — but there is nothing to
	//     clear ahead of time, and the overwhelming majority were already contested by the SYNTHESIZED
	//     surface these declarations replace.
	//   • None of the seven IMPORTS anything.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **THE READINESS PROBE IS THE ONE THIS FILE PRESCRIBES AND IT IS ALSO THE ONE `STDLIB-BRINGUP.md`
	// PROVED VACUOUS, SO IT IS NOT THE EVIDENCE HERE.** `maxon-shv2 build stdlib/Array.maxon` and
	// `… helpers/sort/driftsort.maxon` each exit 1 with `E3001: No 'main' function found` AND NOTHING ELSE —
	// but every one of the six helpers is a conditional `extension`, the exact shape whose bodies the
	// compiler declines to specialize and therefore never analyzes, so that green is worth little. What
	// replaces it is far stronger than the injection control that criterion usually needs: **`array-sort.md`
	// drives these bodies 41 times and passes 41/41** (0/41 against the tree without this entry), and
	// `arr.sort()` on `5,1,4,2,3` prints `12345` and exits 0. A body that runs correctly 41 times was
	// compiled.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **THE DEBT THIS ENTRY CAME DUE ON, AND PAID: `ArrayError`'s SEED.** `Project.maxon` had recorded,
	// in advance and by name, that *"`ArrayError` moves the day `stdlib/Array.maxon` is listed"* — and the
	// reason is not tidiness. `SignatureIndex.ArrayErrorTypeName` is the BARE `b"ArrayError"`, so the parsed
	// declaration and the compiler's seed land on ONE registry key, `upsertDeclaredEnum` is last-wins, and
	// the program runs on the CORPUS layout while `verifyManagedMemoryRuntimeOrdinals` went on checking a rebuilt
	// copy of the seed against itself. MEASURED RED: swapping the two cases in `stdlib/Array.maxon:7-8` made
	// `array-slots.raw-open-slot-reports-empty-slot` answer **99 for an expected 42** and its twin **42 for
	// an expected 99** — the handler arms trading places — with the drift guard emitting NOTHING. The seed is
	// retired and the guard reads the declaration (`overDeclared`). ⚠ **The same sentence had already come
	// due UNNOTICED for `StringError` and `MapError`** — W17 and W41 listed their declaring modules and left
	// both seeds standing — so all three are retired together. ⇒ **listing a module that declares a builtin
	// error enum is not complete until that family's seed is retired and its guard re-pointed.**
	//
	// ⚠ **THE THIRD QUESTION `S2g` ESTABLISHED — what does the entry cost a program that does NOT use it?**
	// MEASURED, same worktree, two binaries differing only by the seven lines below, on
	// `function main() returns ExitCode / return 7`: **1,592 code bytes both ways — Δ0**, and the two
	// executables are **byte-for-byte identical** (5,567 bytes). The two top-level `let`s cost nothing,
	// which is `S2h`'s term working on its third module.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **WHAT IT DOES MOVE IS GOLDENS, AND THAT IS THE POINT OF THE ENTRY RATHER THAN A SIDE EFFECT.**
	// MEASURED by NAMED LISTS against a base built from source in this same worktree — not by `git status`,
	// which cannot answer the question: **65 committed fragments newly drift and 1 stops** (1,449 ⇒ 1,513 on
	// the runner's count, the base's 1,449 being inherited from the merge base). They are exactly the
	// families that would notice `Array`'s emitted code changing: `for-in`/iteration (`for-in-iteration`,
	// `for-in-nested`, `iterate`, `used-for-in-variable`, `rc-for-in-elem-decrefed`), array literals and
	// defaults, float-element arrays, the nine `witness-closure-inheritance` cases, the ten
	// `associated-types` ones, and `sha256.empty-length` — every one of them a program whose `Array` code
	// now comes from a compiled body instead of a hand-built `StdOp` graph. The one that STOPS drifting is
	// `error.array-roster-names-managed-and-not-create`, whose probe this rung re-pointed.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE CORPUS PAID FOR IT IN EXPECTATIONS, ALL SIX MEASURED AND REWRITTEN RATHER THAN SUPPRESSED.**
	// Four `borrow-liveness` cases refused a `for … in` element write that is now SAFE — the loop element is
	// a `+1` copy through `ArrayIterator.current()` and `coOwnBorrowedOpaque`, not a borrow — and are runtime
	// cases pinning 53/53/44/53 with their exit codes. Two `generic-types` cases said *"shv2 reads no
	// stdlib"* and now get `E3018 type 'Array' has no field named 'value'`, which points at the field token
	// and agrees with the oracle's `E4006`. ⛔ One is SHELVED and says why at length
	// (`array-conditional-conformance-withheld.error.a-map-key-array-is-refused-for-its-element`): the opaque
	// COPY GATE fires inside `Array.clone`'s own body and its message carries the ABSOLUTE checkout path,
	// which no portable expectation can pin. That gate is NOT redundant and must not be removed —
	// `Cloneable` existential dispatch reaches the body with no concretely-gated call site anywhere, measured.
	try listWhitelistedModule(stdlibDir, relative: "helpers/sort/insertionSort.maxon", into: listed)
	try listWhitelistedModule(stdlibDir, relative: "helpers/sort/smallSort.maxon", into: listed)
	try listWhitelistedModule(stdlibDir, relative: "helpers/sort/mergeSort.maxon", into: listed)
	try listWhitelistedModule(stdlibDir, relative: "helpers/sort/driftQuicksort.maxon", into: listed)
	try listWhitelistedModule(stdlibDir, relative: "helpers/sort/pdqsort.maxon", into: listed)
	try listWhitelistedModule(stdlibDir, relative: "helpers/sort/driftsort.maxon", into: listed)
	try listWhitelistedModule(stdlibDir, relative: "Array.maxon", into: listed)

	// ⭐⭐ **W153 — `stdlib/List.maxon`, AND THE ENTRY IS THE SMALLER HALF OF ITS OWN RUNG.** The `Set`(W90) /
	// `Map`(W41) / `CharacterSet`(W115) chain, one container over: shv2 SYNTHESIZED `List`, so this line on its
	// own would have been INERT — `BATCH36`'s finding, and the thing `W63`, `W86` and `W112` each built GREEN
	// and then WITHDREW on. What makes it mean anything is the retirement that landed with it: the base name
	// left `ProgramSignatures.isBuiltinGenericBaseNameBesidesTheRecord`, and `isListBaseName` /
	// `isListInstance` / `isListInstanceAt`, `Parser.dispatchListMethod`, the `for … in` node-cursor form and
	// the `__list_from` construction went with it.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE ACCEPTANCE WAS THE DIFFERING-DECLARATIONS CONTROL AND NOT A GREEN SUITE.** With `count()`
	// rewritten to `return chain.count() + 100`, a program printing `l.count()` over a three-element list
	// answered **3** before this entry and **103** after it. The positive control was re-run in the same
	// worktree against `stdlib/Array.maxon` (a reversed `sort()` comparator moving `[10 20 30]` to
	// `[30 20 10]`), because a negative probe nobody has seen fire proves nothing.
	//
	// ⚠ **ITS APPARENT BLOCKER WAS A STALE MEASUREMENT.** `ListRuntime.maxon`'s header said shv2 has *"no
	// `Iterable`/associated-type machinery, so `stdlib/List.maxon`'s `createIterator`/`ListIterator` pair has
	// nothing to plug into"*, which reads as a bar to listing. `stdlib/Array.maxon` is listed and live while
	// declaring `Iterable with (Element, ArrayIter)`: the conformance is CHECKED without being the dispatch
	// route, and `for … in` reaches a declared iterable through `Parser.materializeIterableIterationSource`.
	// The sentence was about DISPATCH, never about listability.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE COLLISION RULE, CLEARED:** it declares no free function at all — every member is on `List` or on
	// `ListIterator` — so it can shadow no bare-name builtin. Its three type names are `List` (compiler-owned
	// as a SPELLING, admitted from the corpus by `recordStruct`'s provenance bit exactly as `Set` / `Map` /
	// `Array` are), `ListIterator`, which no other listed module declares, and the `ListError` enum. It
	// consumes `__ManagedList` / `__ManagedListNode`, which stay compiler-owned and are reached the way
	// `stdlib/Array.maxon` reaches `__ManagedMemory`. It has no top-level binding, so the `S2h` reachability
	// term has nothing of its to keep or drop, and no imports.
	//
	// ⛔ **`Compiler/Runtime/ListRuntime.maxon` STAYS STANDING, DELIBERATELY.** It still serves the
	// `__ManagedList` / `__ManagedListNode` surface this module is written OVER, so most of it is live rather
	// than orphaned; what the retirement stranded is the `List`-only half — CENSUSED at the W153 review by
	// asking which name constant `ListRuntime.maxon` exports has no reader OUTSIDE that file, which is exactly
	// "nothing can emit a call to it": **`__list_from`, `__list_get`, `__list_insert`, `__list_first`,
	// `__list_last`, `__list_remove_at`, `__list_remove_first`, `__list_remove_last`, `__list_head`,
	// `__list_node_next`** — ten, and their builders plus whatever falls out of them
	// (`buildListFrom`/`Get`/`Insert`/`First`/`Last`/`RemoveAt`/`RemoveFirst`/`RemoveLast`/`Head`/`NodeNext`,
	// and `buildListEndAccessor`/`buildListEndRemove`, which serve nothing else).
	//
	// ⛔ **THE ROSTER HERE FIRST READ `__list_append`/`__list_prepend` AND OMITTED `__list_head`/
	// `__list_node_next`, AND BOTH HALVES WERE WRONG** — the two insertions are STILL emitted, by
	// `Parser.dispatchManagedListMethod`'s `insertFirst`/`insertLast` arms, and the two cursor primitives were
	// stranded by the deletion of the `for … in` node-cursor form rather than by the method surface. A roster
	// written from what a rung DELETED is not the same list as one derived from what still has a reader.
	// ⚠ **`isThrowingListRuntimeCallee` and `listAccessorSourceMethodName` still name seven of the ten**, so
	// the deletion rung must strike them there too or `installListRuntime`'s drift guard will do it for them.
	// `W90` left `SetRuntime.maxon` standing for exactly this reason and
	// `W105` deleted it separately, on BYTE-IDENTITY rather than on a green suite — a green suite says nothing
	// BROKE, and only byte-identity says nothing was LIVE.
	try listWhitelistedModule(stdlibDir, relative: "List.maxon", into: listed)

	//     (n) `stdlib/Range.maxon` — W62 (BATCH36). Its ONLY blocker was `W60`: `RangeIterator implements
	//     Iterator with RangeBound, BidirectionalIterator`, where `BidirectionalIterator extends Iterator`,
	//     had `Iterator`'s inherited `current()` checked with NO binding, so a conformance that supplied
	//     `RangeBound` on a sibling entry read `expected current() returns Element`. With that cured the
	//     module probes `E3001` and nothing else.
	//
	//     ⭐ **THE READINESS IS REAL, NOT VACUOUS, AND IT WAS CHECKED THE WAY THIS FILE'S HEADER DEMANDS.**
	//     `Range.maxon` is nominal types with ordinary methods rather than a conditional `extension`, so the
	//     criterion is not structurally blind here — but that was MEASURED and not assumed:
	//     `let bogus = NoSuchType.definitelyUndefined(1)` injected into `RangeIterator.current()`'s body
	//     answers `E3001` **+ `E3004`**, which is the control the six `helpers/sort/*` files fail.
	try listWhitelistedModule(stdlibDir, relative: "Range.maxon", into: listed)

	// ⭐⭐ **W69 — `stdlib/Json.maxon`, THE LARGEST PURE-CORPUS MODULE ON THIS LIST (1,080 lines) AND THE
	// FIRST THAT IS A SUBSYSTEM RATHER THAN A HANDFUL OF LEAVES.** `Json.parse` is a recursive-descent
	// parser (`JsonParser`) filling a node ARENA (`JsonDoc` over a `JsonNodeArray`); `Json.stringify` and
	// `stringifyPrettyNode` are 22 free emitter functions over a `ByteArray`. No intrinsic, no backend work
	// and no `#if`, so the module is loadable exactly as written.
	//
	// ⚠ Its last two blockers cleared UPSTREAM rather than here, and neither was this entry's work: `W66`
	// cured the field access through `doc`, a struct-typed FIELD (`:281`), and *a durable store CO-OWNS*
	// cured the `E2015 :400:21` behind it. Re-probed before listing rather than inherited.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **THE READINESS IS REAL IN BOTH OF THE WAYS THIS FILE HAS LEARNED TO DOUBT IT**, which is two
	// separate doubts and not one: a conditional `extension`'s bodies are never ANALYZED (the six
	// `helpers/sort/*` files), and a name the compiler SYNTHESIZES out-votes the declaration silently
	// (`Set`, `Vector`, `unicodeCategory` — `W63`/`BATCH36`). Both were MEASURED:
	//   • the probe: `maxon-shv2 build stdlib/Json.maxon` exits 1 with `E3001: No 'main' function found`
	//     AND NOTHING ELSE;
	//   • the INJECTION control FIRES — `let bogus = NoSuchType.definitelyUndefined(1)` in
	//     `findKeyInNode`'s body answers `E3001` **+ `E3004 call to undefined function
	//     'NoSuchType.definitelyUndefined'`**, run on a copy outside `stdlib/`;
	//   • there is NO SYNTHESIZED TWIN: `Json` is on neither `TypeResolution.isCompilerOwnedTypeName` nor
	//     `builtinTypeNameTag`, and it is none of the seven `*BuiltinBaseName` roots (`Set`, `Map`, `List`,
	//     `Vector`, `__ManagedList`, `__ManagedListNode`, `__ManagedMemoryCursor`). shv2's own sources
	//     mention the name in PROSE only.
	//
	// It clears every entry in this file's collision rule, each CHECKED rather than assumed:
	//   • ⚠⚠ FREE FUNCTIONS (E3006) — **the dangerous clause, and this is the first entry with a large
	//     surface for it: TWENTY-TWO**, several as generic as a name gets (`writeBool`, `writeNumber`,
	//     `writeBytes`, `writeIndent`, `hexDigit`). Checked at BOTH surfaces the rule names, and at their
	//     SOURCES rather than against any prose roster. Against `Parser.parseCallNamed`'s bare-name chain
	//     AT THE CHAIN: the seventeen names standing there today are `calleeTakesPositionalArgsOnly`'s
	//     eight (`trunc` `floor` `ceil` `round` `sqrt` `abs` `min` `max`), `runProcess`, `spawnReadLine`
	//     and the seven `subp*` — and none of the 22 is among them. Against the free functions the 37
	//     already-listed modules declare, read out of those files: 122 declarations, and the intersection
	//     with these 22 is EMPTY.
	//   • ⚠ It CONSUMES one, which is the sound direction and what the builtins are for: `JsonDoc.getInt`
	//     (`:171`) and `writeNumber` (`:1027`) call `trunc` by bare name, exactly as `Math.pow` calls
	//     `floor`. What a bare builtin reserves is a DECLARATION no call site can reach, and this module
	//     declares no `trunc`.
	//   • TYPE NAMES: `Json`, `JsonDoc`, `JsonNode`, `JsonKind`, `JsonParser`, `JsonByte`, `JsonError`,
	//     `JsonAccessError` — eight, all nominal, so a user program spelling one wins and this module's
	//     moves into the reserved space (A1s wave 2). No other stdlib file declares any of them, checked
	//     AT THE SOURCE across all 50. TYPE-NAME-vs-BUILTIN, the one shape still SILENT, is the
	//     no-synthesized-twin bullet above — the same question asked of the three rosters that answer it.
	//   • ⚠ TYPEALIASES — twelve, and **FOUR ARE ALREADY DECLARED BY LISTED MODULES**: `Byte` and
	//     `ByteArray` (`File`, `Console`, `String`, `Character`, `views`), `BytePos` (`String`) and
	//     `StringArray` (`CommandLine`, `Build`, `String`). Every one at the IDENTICAL FORM —
	//     `int(0 to u8.max)`, `Array with Byte`, `int(0 to u64.max)`, `Array with String` — so
	//     `RangedAliasRegistry`'s whole-program fold has no range to contest. That registry's own header
	//     already names this file in the seven that *"declare `Byte = int(0 to u8.max)` privately … and
	//     agree"*. The other eight (`JsonNodeId`, `JsonNodeIdArray`, `JsonNodeArray`, `ChildCount`,
	//     `ChildIndex`, `IndentDepth`, `JsonInt`, `JsonFloat`) are declared nowhere else under `stdlib/`.
	//   • ⚠ `StringArray` is the first TOP-LEVEL `export`ed declaration of that name on the list — the
	//     three above declare it inside a type body. A difference in SCOPE, not in FORM, and
	//     `Project.isStdlibModuleDirectory`'s header already anticipates it BY NAME: a project's own
	//     `export typealias StringArray` shadows `stdlib/Json.maxon`'s rather than colliding with it
	//     (`typealias-collision/project-export-shadows-stdlib-export`).
	//   • ONE TOP-LEVEL BINDING, `let firstPrintableByte = 0x20 as Byte` (`:803`) — the class `S2h` had to
	//     build a reachability term for after `Log.maxon`'s mutable state cost a trivial program +87%.
	//     `DeadGlobalElimination` drops it: the A/B below emits no `.data` slot for it and no
	//     `__module_init` store, which is that term working rather than the prediction that an immutable
	//     scalar is cheaper than a `var`.
	//   • The "type-parameterized by a USER declaration" clause is the one this file insists is a BUILD and
	//     not a reading, and it bites hardest here because `Byte`/`ByteArray` are the very names it is
	//     about. Checked as a build: a program declaring `typealias Byte = int(0 to 1000)` and calling
	//     nothing in this module compiles and runs clean.
	//   • E3112 fires only when a user program actually contests a spelling, so there is nothing to clear
	//     ahead of time. The surface it exposes is `JsonNode`'s six fields (`kind`, `boolValue`,
	//     `numberValue`, `stringValue`, `keys`, `children`) and `JsonDoc`'s two (`nodes`, `root`), the
	//     method names, and the parameter names those methods take as labels (`key`, `index`, `children`,
	//     `depth`, `out`, `doc`, `id`, `ids`, `keys`).
	//   • It has NO imports and calls into no other stdlib module by name.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **THE THIRD QUESTION `S2g` ESTABLISHED — what does the entry cost a program that does NOT use
	// it? — CAME BACK RED, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE STORY OF THIS RUNG.** MEASURED first, before anything was
	// cured: with only the line below added, `function main() returns ExitCode / return 7` kept its 1,592
	// code bytes but grew a `.rdata` SECTION IT HAD NEVER HAD (three PE sections to four, 5,567 ⇒ 6,115
	// bytes on disk) and **13 committed goldens shifted by exactly one label** — including
	// `stdlib-whitelist.a-listed-modules-literals-are-byte-neutral`. The payload was eight zero bytes at id
	// 0, referenced by nothing.
	//
	// ⛔ **THAT CASE DID NOT GO RED, AND THIS ENTRY IMPLIED IT HAD (W69 review).** It reads a displaced
	// payload off its golden FRAGMENT, and a fragment mismatch is REFERENCE, NOT A GATE. MEASURED on a
	// compiler rebuilt with the gate neutralised: **PASS**, exit 0, with the orphan at `.rdata` byte 0 of
	// every program in the suite. What found this was the 1,228-fragment DRIFT COUNT below — a number a
	// human reads. The review added `a-listed-modules-literals-cannot-reach-the-rdata-image`, which pins the
	// linked `.rdata` from byte 0 with a ```RequiredRdata block and is the first thing in the battery that
	// turns red if the gate is removed.
	//
	// ⭐ **IT WAS NOT THIS MODULE'S FAULT, AND THE BISECT IS WHY THE ENTRY STANDS RATHER THAN BEING
	// WITHDRAWN.** `stdlib/` is read as DATA, so the carrier was found without a rebuild: temporarily
	// spelling `:77`'s `export var stringValue as String = ""` as `= "ZQ"` turned the mystery payload into
	// `ZQ\0\0\0\0\0\0`. A STRING field default is not a `FieldDefault.literal` (that payload is a
	// `ParsedInt`), so it mints the nullary helper `__fieldDefault#JsonNode#stringValue` — and
	// `LowerMaxonToStd.registerProgramLiteralBlobs` walked that helper with **no unreachable-stdlib gate**,
	// where the two passes on either side of it have carried one for exactly this reason since A1s. It is
	// the THIRD pre-elimination door onto `GlobalDataTable.nextStringId`, and `InsertRangeChecks`'s header
	// claimed the class was shut at two. Gating it is `W69`'s one compiler change; with it, the A/B above
	// is **1,592 code bytes both ways and the two executables byte-for-byte identical at 5,567 bytes**.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **AND THE GATE IS WORTH FAR MORE THAN THIS ENTRY: IT REMOVED ORPHANS THAT WERE ALREADY THERE, IN
	// NEARLY EVERY PROGRAM IN THE SUITE.** MEASURED as a DELTA against a base built from source in this
	// same worktree — not by `git status`, which cannot answer the question: the base drifts on **0** of
	// 5,573 committed fragments and the tip on **1,228** of 5,575. Every one of them is a pure
	// `.rdata` LABEL RENUMBER and every one goes DOWNWARDS (`advent/day4a`: `__str_rec_127` ⇒
	// `__str_rec_1`; `abs.rt-float`: `__str_rec_135` ⇒ `__str_rec_46`) — the first-difference pairs
	// compared with their label suffixes erased came out **all identical and 0 structural**. Not one instruction
	// in the suite changed. ⇒ that is dead `.rdata` leaving the image of every program that reaches stdlib
	// at all, a cost the tree has been paying since the first listed module with an unreachable
	// literal-bearing body.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE SLOTS REMOVED VASTLY OUTNUMBER THE BYTES, and the reason is DEDUP — do not read one as the
	// other.** `registerRdataConstant` pools by bytes, so an orphan blob that matches a live one costs a
	// COUNTER SLOT and no image space at all. MEASURED on `let s = "hello world"` + one `print`: `.rdata`
	// 2,704 ⇒ 2,656 bytes (the file unchanged at 18,877, section alignment absorbing it) against label
	// indices that move by tens. The win this gate is FOR is the invariant, not the bytes.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE CAPABILITY IS REAL RATHER THAN MERELY LOADABLE, and for a module this size that is the
	// point.** `specs-shv2/stdlib-whitelist.md`'s two `json-*` cases are the differing-declarations control
	// in spec form: one parses a document and reads five values back out through the arena (its last read
	// NEGATIVE, so a `findKeyInNode` answering with any id would fail rather than pass), the other builds
	// an arena by hand and serializes it, checking the emitted text literally and then re-parsing it. Both
	// are oracle-agreed on the identical source, and neither can pass against an inert entry. Neither
	// carries a `targets:` restriction and both were RUN on `wasm32-wasi`, not merely left unrestricted.
	try listWhitelistedModule(stdlibDir, relative: "Json.maxon", into: listed)

	// ⭐⭐ **W81 — `stdlib/Testing.maxon`, THE ASSERTION LIBRARY, AND THE FIRST MODULE WHOSE ADMISSION TOOK
	// THREE RUNGS OF LANGUAGE WORK RATHER THAN ANY WORK OF ITS OWN.** 343 lines, no intrinsic, no backend
	// work, no `#if`: one `enum TestFailure`, one `type __TestReport`, and `type Expect`'s twenty-two
	// `static` matchers. What kept it off the list was not the module — it was that shv2 could not yet
	// COMPILE what it is written in. `W72` opened the `__` declaration door it needs for `__TestReport`,
	// `W74` gave each member of an overload set its own parameter defaults (`equal` has three arms and every
	// one of them defaults `message`/`file`/`line`), and `W77` admitted an overload set whose members agree
	// on `throws TestFailure`. None of those three rungs was allowed to list it; this one does.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **THE READINESS IS REAL IN BOTH OF THE WAYS THIS FILE HAS LEARNED TO DOUBT IT.** Both MEASURED:
	//   • the probe: `maxon-shv2 build stdlib/Testing.maxon` exits 1 with `E3001: No 'main' function found`
	//     AND NOTHING ELSE;
	//   • the INJECTION control FIRES — `let bogus = NoSuchType.definitelyUndefined(1)` in
	//     `__TestReport.threw`'s body answers `E3001` **+ `E3004 call to undefined function
	//     'NoSuchType.definitelyUndefined'` at `stdlib/Testing.maxon:53:15`**, so its bodies are analyzed;
	//   • there is NO SYNTHESIZED TWIN, the hazard that made `Set`/`Vector`/`unicodeCategory` INERT when
	//     listed: none of the seven `*BuiltinBaseName` roots in `SignatureIndex.maxon` (`Set`, `Map`, `List`,
	//     `ManagedList`, `ManagedListNode`, `ManagedMemoryCursor`, `Vector` — named rather than cited by line,
	//     since this entry's line range was already stale by six) names any of them, and no
	//     `type`/`enum`/`typealias` DECLARATION
	//     anywhere under `maxon-shv2/Compiler/` spells `TestFailure`, `__TestReport` or `Expect`.
	//     ⚠ **STATED AS A DECLARATION SWEEP AND NOT AS A GREP, BECAUSE THE GREP DOES NOT COME BACK EMPTY AND
	//     THIS ENTRY SAID IT DID** (W81 review). A plain `grep b"Testing" b"Expect" b"TestFailure"
	//     b"__TestReport"` over `Compiler/` hits `Queries.TestReportDeclaringModule`, every `Expected '…'`
	//     diagnostic in the lexer and parser, and every `Testing/ladders/…` citation — a dozen files, on
	//     `main`, before this rung. The CONCLUSION is unchanged and the roots check is what carries it; what
	//     is corrected is the evidence, which a later reader would have run and disbelieved.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **THE CONTROL HAD TO BE RUN IN PLACE, AND `STDLIB-BRINGUP.md`'s RECIPE ANSWERS THE WRONG QUESTION
	// FOR THIS MODULE.** That recipe copies the module OUTSIDE `stdlib/` before injecting, which for this one
	// dies at `E2051: identifier '__TestReport' is reserved` before reaching any body — `W72`'s door admits a
	// `__` declaration only UNDER `stdlib/`. So the injection is made in place, against a backup, and the
	// tree restored (`git status stdlib/` clean) after. A later reader re-running the control owes the same.
	//
	// It clears every entry in this file's collision rule, each CHECKED at the source rather than asserted:
	//   • ⚠⚠ FREE FUNCTIONS (E3006), the dangerous clause — **ZERO, and an empty clause checked is worth
	//     writing down.** Every one of its 25 functions is a member of `__TestReport` or `Expect`; a
	//     top-level `function`/`export function` count over the file is 0. So there is nothing to contest
	//     `Parser.parseCallNamed`'s bare-name chain and nothing to intersect with the free functions the
	//     already-listed modules declare, and neither surface needed enumerating.
	//   • ⚠ It CONSUMES one, which is the sound direction: `__TestReport.threw` (`:53`) and `Expect.raise`
	//     (`:150`) call `printError` by bare name, out of LISTED `stdlib/PrintError.maxon`, and `Expect.close`
	//     (`:287`) calls the `abs` builtin. What a bare builtin reserves is a DECLARATION no call site can
	//     reach, and this module declares neither.
	//   • TYPE NAMES: `TestFailure`, `__TestReport`, `Expect` — three, all nominal, so a user program
	//     spelling one wins and this module's moves into the reserved space (A1s wave 2). No other file
	//     under `stdlib/` declares any of them, checked at the source across all 50 (`find stdlib -name
	//     '*.maxon' | wc -l` — this entry said 51; re-counted at the W81 review).
	//   • TYPEALIASES: `AssertedInt` (`int(i64.min to i64.max)`), `AssertedReal` (`float(f64.min to f64.max)`)
	//     and `Tolerance` (`float(0.0 to f64.max)`) — declared nowhere else under `stdlib/`, so
	//     `RangedAliasRegistry`'s whole-program fold has no range to contest. It USES a fourth,
	//     `SourceLineNumber` (`stdlib/Builtins.maxon:208`, `int(1 to i32.max)`), from a LISTED module and at
	//     that module's own form — the sound direction, and the one the `__line__` default depends on.
	//   • TOP-LEVEL BINDINGS: **two `var`s with STRING initializers** (`:31 wireWrapper`, `:32 wireSeparator`),
	//     which is the class that cost `Log.maxon` +87% code bytes on a program that never spells `Log` and
	//     got that entry withdrawn. The A/B below is this clause's answer and it is CLEAN: `S2h`'s
	//     `DeadGlobalElimination` reachability term plus `W69`'s `.rdata` gate DO reach a `var` holding a
	//     String.
	//   • The "type-parameterized by a USER declaration" clause is the one this file insists is a BUILD and
	//     not a reading. Checked as a build: a program declaring its own `typealias Tolerance = int(0 to 10)`,
	//     `typealias AssertedInt = int(0 to 5)`, `type Expect` and `enum TestFailure`, calling nothing in this
	//     module, compiles and runs clean and gets its OWN declarations everywhere.
	//   • E3112 fires only when a user program actually contests a spelling, so there is nothing to clear
	//     ahead of time. The surface it exposes is `Expect`'s twenty-two matcher names, its fourteen `static
	//     let` report-vocabulary constants, `__TestReport`'s two members, and the parameter names those
	//     matchers take as labels (`expected`, `than`, `within`, `needle`, `message`, `file`, `line`).
	//   • It has NO imports and calls into no listed module by name except `printError`.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **ONE FACT THIS ENTRY RECORDS THAT NO EARLIER ONE HAD TO: HALF THE MODULE HAS NO CALLER IN shv2
	// YET, AND THAT IS A STATE TO STATE RATHER THAN A GAP TO FILL HERE.** `Queries.TestReportDeclaringModule`
	// (`Queries.maxon:1229`) already names this file — the compiler knows the module by name — but shv2 has
	// **no `test` declaration syntax at all** (`grep b"test"` over `Compiler/` is empty). `__TestReport.threw`
	// is documented in the corpus as *"the compiler emits this call; no program writes it"*, and in shv2
	// nothing emits it. So `__TestReport` is listed, parsed, analyzed and then dropped by
	// `DeadFunctionElimination` in every program. **`Expect` is the surface this listing actually delivers**,
	// and the `test` declaration that gives `__TestReport` its caller is a later rung's.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE CAPABILITY IS REAL RATHER THAN MERELY LOADABLE.** `specs-shv2/testing-assertions.md` is the
	// canonical spec ported BYTE-IDENTICAL, and all 11 of its cases pass with the entry live — every matcher
	// on its holding path, every matcher on its failing path against a literal stderr golden, `__file__`/
	// `__line__` reported at the CALLER's line and across files, `equal` resolving across its three overloads
	// from a method call, and the two compile-error cases. None is disabled and none can pass against an
	// inert entry.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **AND IT COST FIVE COMPILER DEFECTS, FOUR OF WHICH ARE LIVE ON `main` AND HAVE NOTHING TO DO WITH
	// THIS MODULE.** Each is argued where it was fixed: `StdlibSource.markReachable` (a minted
	// parameter-default helper is as PROVISIONAL as the base callee beside it and only the base was widened,
	// so every overload member's default helper but the FIRST tripped `requireUnreachableStdlibStayedDead`);
	// `Parser.enumInterpRendering` (an enum rendered its raw i64 in every interpolation, so a plain enum
	// printed its ORDINAL, a string-backed one printed `0`, a character-backed one its CODEPOINT and a
	// payload-bearing union a HEAP ADDRESS — six rows measured against the oracle, five of them wrong);
	// `Parser.parseCallNamed` (a QUALIFIED call anchored every diagnostic at the TYPE token where the
	// instance spelling and both references use the MEMBER token); `SemanticCheck.checkArgTypes` (one call
	// earned one diagnostic per bad argument where the reference emits the first); and
	// `ProgramSignatures.diagnosticCalleeSpelling` (a resolved stdlib callee was named bare where canonical
	// names its MODULE). The listing is what reached them; not one was reachable from a spec this suite
	// already ran.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE LAST OF THE FIVE IS FIXED FOR ONE DIAGNOSTIC AND OWES FOUR MORE**, and that is stated at
	// `diagnosticCalleeSpelling` rather than here: the same rule puts E3055, E3008, E3088 and E3036 on the
	// qualified side, and moving them is a tree-wide naming policy with ~50 committed expectations behind it.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE `S2h` BYTE-NEUTRALITY A/B, RE-MEASURED ON THIS RUNG'S FINAL COMPILER** — same worktree, two
	// binaries differing only by the line below, on `function main() returns ExitCode / return 7`:
	// **1,592 bytes of code both ways**, and the two executables are **byte-for-byte identical at 5,567
	// bytes**. The two String-initialized `var`s cost a program that never spells `Expect` nothing at all.
	try listWhitelistedModule(stdlibDir, relative: "Testing.maxon", into: listed)

	// ⛔⛔ **A `stdlib/Set.maxon` IS NOT LISTED` PARAGRAPH STOOD HERE UNTIL W105, AND IT HAD BEEN FALSE SINCE
	// W90 LISTED THE MODULE 390 LINES ABOVE.** It was a W63/BATCH36 measurement — the entry was added, built
	// green, and WITHDRAWN, because with the synthesized `Set` still winning every query the listing bought
	// nothing: `Set.count()` rewritten to `return count + 100` still answered **2**, and an UNDEFINED CALL
	// injected into that body compiled clean. The withdrawal's own closing line named the condition that
	// lifted it (*"listing waits on the synthesized `Set` being retired in favour of the declaration"*), W90
	// met it, and nothing came back to strike the paragraph. Its lesson survives where it is still true —
	// this file's header rule that a whitelist entry which changes no emitted byte and survives its own
	// module's bodies being sabotaged is not a cone entry — and the dated claim goes.

	return listed
end 'whitelistedStdlibModules'

// One listed module. A listed module the checkout does not have is a hard error naming it, never a
// silent skip: the whole point of the list is that what it names is present.
//
// It raises the SAME `fileNotFound` a user's own missing input raises, and deliberately not a
// scaffolding-specific arm of its own. A private error case would be a second thing that has to be
// deleted the day the filter goes — in `Diagnostics.maxon` and again in `Main.maxon`'s printer —
// which is exactly the "one mention, one place" property this file exists to keep. The fact is the
// same either way: a path this compile was told to load is not there.
function listWhitelistedModule(stdlibDir FilePath, relative String, into MaxonSourcePathSet) throws CompileError
	let path = stdlibDir.join(relative)
	if not File.exists(path) 'missing'
		throw CompileError.fileNotFound(path)
	end 'missing'

	into.insert(path)
end 'listWhitelistedModule'
